window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-bio-neural","kind":"practice","exam":"NEET","year":null,"title":"Neural Control and Coordination · Practice","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","subject":"Biology","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-001","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":1,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Within a fraction of a second after being excited, a site on the axon becomes responsive to further stimulation.\nReason (R): The rise in $Na^+$ permeability is short-lived and is followed by $K^+$ diffusing out, which restores the resting potential.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that the fibre becomes once more responsive to further stimulation.\nR is true. The $Na^+$ permeability rise is extremely short-lived and is quickly followed by $K^+$ efflux.\nR explains A, because restoring the resting potential is exactly what allows the site to respond again.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-002","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":2,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The midbrain receives and integrates visual, tactile and auditory inputs.\nStatement II: The cerebellum is one of the three regions that make up the brain stem.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT states that the midbrain receives and integrates visual, tactile and auditory inputs.\nStatement II is incorrect. The brain stem consists of the midbrain, pons and medulla oblongata. The cerebellum is part of the hindbrain but lies outside the brain stem.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-003","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":3,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Read the two statements about a resting neuron.\nStatement I: The axoplasm contains a high concentration of $K^+$ and negatively charged proteins, and a low concentration of $Na^+$.\nStatement II: The fluid outside the axon contains a high concentration of $K^+$ and a low concentration of $Na^+$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Inside the axon, $K^+$ and negatively charged proteins are high and $Na^+$ is low.\nStatement II is incorrect. The extracellular fluid has low $K^+$ and high $Na^+$, the opposite of the axoplasm.\nThis difference between inside and outside is the concentration gradient.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-004","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":4,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"A head injury damages the part of the brain that contains centres controlling respiration, cardiovascular reflexes and gastric secretions. Which option correctly classifies this damaged part?","image":"","options":["Forebrain; part of the limbic system","Midbrain; part of the brain stem","Hindbrain; not part of the brain stem","Hindbrain; part of the brain stem"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Step 1: The centres for respiration, cardiovascular reflexes and gastric secretions lie in the medulla oblongata.\nStep 2: The medulla belongs to the hindbrain, along with the pons and cerebellum.\nStep 3: The medulla is also one of the three brain stem regions (midbrain, pons, medulla).\nOption (3) fails because the medulla is part of the brain stem.\nOption (2) fails because the midbrain does not contain these centres.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-005","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":5,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) Cell body\n(b) Dendrites\n(c) Axon\n(d) Synaptic knob\nList-II:\n(i) Long fibre carrying impulses away from the cell body\n(ii) Bulb-like ending containing synaptic vesicles\n(iii) Contains typical cell organelles along with Nissl's granules\n(iv) Short, repeatedly branched fibres carrying impulses towards the cell body","image":"","options":["a-(iii), b-(iv), c-(i), d-(ii)","a-(iv), b-(iii), c-(ii), d-(i)","a-(iii), b-(i), c-(iv), d-(ii)","a-(ii), b-(iv), c-(i), d-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) The cell body has cytoplasm with typical organelles and Nissl's granules, so a-(iii).\n(b) Dendrites are short, branched fibres that carry impulses towards the cell body, so b-(iv).\n(c) The axon is a long fibre that carries impulses away from the cell body, so c-(i).\n(d) The synaptic knob is a bulb-like ending with synaptic vesicles, so d-(ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-006","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":6,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"In the human brain, the canal called the cerebral aqueduct passes through which region?","image":"","options":["Medulla oblongata","Midbrain","Cerebellum","Thalamus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: the midbrain lies between the thalamus/hypothalamus of the forebrain and the pons of the hindbrain.\nA canal called the cerebral aqueduct passes through the midbrain.\nThe medulla is a tempting choice because it is also part of the brain stem. However, the medulla is a hindbrain region that connects to the spinal cord, and the aqueduct does not run through it.\nThe cerebellum and the thalamus also do not carry this canal.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-007","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":7,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"A drug completely blocks the neurotransmitter receptors on the post-synaptic membrane of a chemical synapse. An action potential then arrives at the pre-synaptic axon terminal. Which outcome is expected?","image":"","options":["The action potential fails to reach the pre-synaptic axon terminal","Synaptic vesicles are unable to fuse with the pre-synaptic membrane","Neurotransmitters are released into the cleft, but no new potential is generated in the post-synaptic neuron","The impulse crosses the synapse as direct electrical current instead"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Events on the pre-synaptic side are unaffected. The action potential arrives, vesicles fuse, and neurotransmitters are released into the cleft.\nThe released neurotransmitters cannot bind their blocked receptors.\nIt is this binding that opens ion channels on the post-synaptic membrane, so the channels stay closed. With no ion entry, no new potential arises.\nOption (4) fails because a chemical synapse has a cleft and cannot switch to direct current flow.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-008","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":8,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The inner surface of a resting axonal membrane is negative because the membrane is impermeable to $K^+$, which traps $K^+$ inside the axon.\nStatement II: In a resting neuron, the sodium-potassium pump moves $Na^+$ from the axoplasm, where its concentration is low, to the outside fluid, where its concentration is high.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. At rest, the membrane is comparatively MORE permeable to $K^+$, not impermeable to it. The negative inner surface comes from the trapped negatively charged proteins and the unequal $Na^+$/$K^+$ distribution kept up by the pump.\nStatement II is correct. $Na^+$ is low inside and high outside. The pump moves it outward against this gradient, which is why active transport is needed.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-009","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":9,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are cranial meninges?\nDura mater, Arachnoid, Corpus callosum, Pia mater, Cerebral aqueduct, Corpora quadrigemina","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Dura mater: a meninx (outer layer).\nArachnoid: a meninx (middle layer).\nPia mater: a meninx (inner layer).\nCorpus callosum: not a meninx; it is a tract of nerve fibres connecting the two cerebral hemispheres.\nCerebral aqueduct: not a meninx; it is a canal passing through the midbrain.\nCorpora quadrigemina: not a meninx; these are four lobes on the dorsal side of the midbrain.\nSo the total is 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-010","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":10,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"In a resting (polarised) axon, how are the charges arranged across the axonal membrane?","image":"","options":["Outer surface negative, inner surface positive","Both surfaces positive","Both surfaces electrically neutral","Outer surface positive, inner surface negative"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"At rest, the outer surface of the axonal membrane carries a positive charge and the inner surface a negative charge. This is why the membrane is called polarised.\nOption (1) describes a depolarised site during an action potential, so it does not apply at rest.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-011","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":11,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Unmyelinated nerve fibres are commonly found in the somatic neural system.\nReason (R): The somatic neural system relays impulses from the CNS to skeletal muscles.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that unmyelinated fibres are commonly found in the autonomous and the somatic neural systems.\nR is true. The somatic system relays impulses from the CNS to skeletal muscles.\nHowever, R only describes what the somatic system does. It says nothing about why its fibres lack a myelin sheath, so it does not explain A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-012","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":12,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"When a neuron is not conducting any impulse, its axonal membrane is comparatively more permeable to which of the following?","image":"","options":["Sodium ions ($Na^+$)","Potassium ions ($K^+$)","Negatively charged proteins of the axoplasm","Calcium ions ($Ca^{2+}$)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"At rest, the axonal membrane is comparatively more permeable to $K^+$.\nIt is nearly impermeable to $Na^+$, so option (1) is wrong.\nIt is impermeable to the negatively charged proteins in the axoplasm, so option (3) is wrong.\nNCERT does not describe $Ca^{2+}$ permeability in the resting state, so option (4) does not fit.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-013","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":13,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Where are the specific receptors for neurotransmitters located at a chemical synapse?","image":"","options":["Membrane of the synaptic vesicles","Pre-synaptic membrane of the axon terminal","Post-synaptic membrane","Schwann cells around the axon"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Neurotransmitters released into the synaptic cleft bind to specific receptors on the post-synaptic membrane.\nThis binding opens ion channels and generates a new potential in the post-synaptic neuron.\nThe pre-synaptic terminal is the side that releases the neurotransmitter; it is not where the transmitter acts.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-014","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":14,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the limbic system is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["It is formed by the inner parts of the cerebral hemispheres together with deep structures such as the amygdala and hippocampus.","Along with the hypothalamus, it is involved in regulating sexual behaviour and emotional reactions such as rage and fear.","It contains groups of neurosecretory cells that secrete hypothalamic hormones.","It is concerned with olfaction, autonomic responses and motivation."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the limbic lobe consists of the inner parts of the hemispheres plus deep structures such as the amygdala and hippocampus.\n(2) Correct: together with the hypothalamus, it regulates sexual behaviour and emotional reactions such as excitement, pleasure, rage and fear.\n(3) Incorrect: the neurosecretory cells that secrete hypothalamic hormones belong to the hypothalamus, not the limbic system.\n(4) Correct: the NCERT summary links the limbic system to olfaction, autonomic responses and motivation.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-015","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":15,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"A stimulus is applied at site A of a polarised axon, and site B lies just ahead of it. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) At site A the membrane becomes freely permeable to $Na^+$, causing a rapid influx of $Na^+$.\n(ii) After depolarisation, the outer surface of the membrane at A becomes negatively charged.\n(iii) On the inner surface of the membrane, current flows from site B to site A.\n(iv) The rise in $Na^+$ permeability is long-lasting, and $K^+$ permeability increases only after several seconds.\n(v) Outward diffusion of $K^+$ restores the resting potential at the site of excitation.","image":"","options":["Two","Four","Three","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the stimulated site becomes freely permeable to $Na^+$, so $Na^+$ rushes in.\n(ii) Correct: the polarity reverses, making the outside negative and the inside positive.\n(iii) Incorrect: on the inner surface current flows from A to B. On the outer surface it flows from B to A.\n(iv) Incorrect: the $Na^+$ permeability rise is extremely short-lived and is quickly followed by a rise in $K^+$ permeability.\n(v) Correct: $K^+$ leaving the axon repolarises the membrane.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-016","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":16,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"The neural system of all animals is composed of highly specialised cells called neurons. What can these cells do?","image":"","options":["Only secrete hormones into the blood","Detect, receive and transmit different kinds of stimuli","Only store energy for muscular activity","Only produce antibodies against stimuli"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes neurons as highly specialised cells that can detect, receive and transmit different kinds of stimuli.\nSecreting hormones into the blood (option 1) is the main feature of endocrine cells.\nOptions (3) and (4) describe roles that have nothing to do with neurons.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-017","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":17,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Multipolar neuron\n(B) Bipolar neuron\n(C) Unipolar neuron\n(D) Myelinated nerve fibre\nList-II\n(I) Retina of eye\n(II) Cranial nerves\n(III) Embryonic stage\n(IV) Cerebral cortex\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Multipolar neurons (one axon, two or more dendrites) are found in the cerebral cortex, so A-IV.\n(B) Bipolar neurons (one axon, one dendrite) are found in the retina, so B-I.\n(C) Unipolar neurons are found usually in the embryonic stage, so C-III.\n(D) Myelinated fibres occur in spinal and cranial nerves, so D-II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-018","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":18,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following structures are components of the human brain stem?\nMidbrain, Pons, Cerebellum, Medulla oblongata, Thalamus, Hypothalamus","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The brain stem is made of three regions: midbrain, pons and medulla oblongata.\nThe cerebellum belongs to the hindbrain but is not part of the brain stem.\nThe thalamus and hypothalamus belong to the forebrain.\nSo three of the listed structures are brain stem components.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-019","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":19,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Rapid $Na^+$ influx\n(b) $K^+$ diffusing outward\n(c) Wave of depolarisation and repolarisation along the axon\n(d) Outer surface positive, inner surface negative at rest\nList-II\n(i) Conduction of nerve impulse\n(ii) Polarised state\n(iii) Depolarisation\n(iv) Repolarisation","image":"","options":["a-(iii), b-(iv), c-(i), d-(ii)","a-(iv), b-(iii), c-(i), d-(ii)","a-(iii), b-(iv), c-(ii), d-(i)","a-(i), b-(iv), c-(iii), d-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) $Na^+$ influx reverses the polarity, which is depolarisation: (iii).\n(b) $K^+$ efflux restores the resting potential, which is repolarisation: (iv).\n(c) NCERT says the impulse is conducted as a wave of depolarisation and repolarisation: (i).\n(d) Positive outside and negative inside is the resting polarised state: (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-020","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":20,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"At a chemical synapse, what are the membranes of the pre-synaptic and post-synaptic neurons separated by?","image":"","options":["Node of Ranvier","Synaptic cleft","Myelin sheath","Corpus callosum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"At a chemical synapse, a fluid-filled space separates the pre-synaptic and post-synaptic membranes. This space is called the synaptic cleft.\nA node of Ranvier is the gap between two adjacent myelin sheaths on an axon, not a space between two neurons.\nThe corpus callosum is a tract of fibres that connects the two cerebral hemispheres.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-021","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":21,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nA. Synaptic knob\nB. Synaptic cleft\nC. Post-synaptic membrane\nD. Electrical synapse\nList-II\n(i) Bears specific receptors for neurotransmitters\n(ii) Bulb-like ending of an axon branch containing vesicles\n(iii) Current flows directly from one neuron into another\n(iv) Fluid-filled space between two neuronal membranes","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(ii): Each axon branch ends in a bulb-like synaptic knob that holds synaptic vesicles.\nB-(iv): The synaptic cleft is the fluid-filled space between the two neuronal membranes.\nC-(i): The receptors for neurotransmitters are located on the post-synaptic membrane.\nD-(iii): At an electrical synapse, current passes directly from one neuron to the other.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-022","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":22,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Which canal passes through the midbrain of humans?","image":"","options":["Central canal","Cerebral aqueduct","Corpus callosum","Third ventricle"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that a canal called the cerebral aqueduct passes through the midbrain.\nThe corpus callosum is not a canal. It is a tract of nerve fibres that connects the two cerebral hemispheres.\nThe central canal runs through the spinal cord, not the midbrain.\nThe third ventricle is a forebrain cavity.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-023","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":23,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Large regions of the cerebral cortex that are neither clearly sensory nor motor in function are called association areas.\nStatement II: The cerebral cortex forms the inner part of the cerebral hemisphere and is made of myelinated fibre tracts.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The cortex has motor areas, sensory areas, and association areas that are neither clearly sensory nor motor.\nStatement II is incorrect. The cortex is the outer covering layer of cells and forms the grey matter. The inner part made of myelinated tracts is the white matter.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-024","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":24,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT a component of the human brain stem?","image":"","options":["Midbrain","Pons","Medulla oblongata","Cerebellum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT states that three regions make up the brain stem: the midbrain, the pons and the medulla oblongata.\nThe brain stem connects the brain with the spinal cord.\nThe cerebellum belongs to the hindbrain together with the pons and medulla. It is not counted as part of the brain stem.\nSo the cerebellum is the odd one out.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-025","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":25,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"An axon is stimulated at point A. Point B lies just ahead of A and is still in the resting state. Which of the following statements correctly describes events during conduction of the impulse from A to B?","image":"","options":["On the outer surface of the membrane, current flows from site B to site A","On the inner surface of the membrane, current flows from site B to site A","The increase in $Na^+$ permeability at site A persists until the impulse reaches the axon terminal","The resting potential at site A is restored mainly by an outward movement of $Na^+$ through ion channels"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"At A, $Na^+$ influx makes the inside positive and the outside negative. B is still outside-positive and inside-negative.\n(2) Wrong: on the inner surface, current flows from A to B.\n(1) Correct: on the outer surface, current flows from B to A, completing the circuit.\n(3) Wrong: the rise in $Na^+$ permeability is extremely short-lived.\n(4) Wrong: the resting potential is restored by $K^+$ diffusing out.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-026","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":26,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"An action potential has just been generated at site A of an axon. Site B lies immediately ahead of it.\nStatement I: On the inner surface of the membrane, current flows from site A to site B.\nStatement II: On the outer surface of the membrane, current also flows from site A to site B.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Inside, site A is positive and site B is negative, so current flows from A to B along the inner surface.\nStatement II is incorrect. Outside, site B is positive and site A is negative, so current flows from B to A.\nThis outer flow completes the circuit of current.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-027","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":27,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Hydra\n(B) Insects\n(C) Vertebrates\n(D) Endocrine system\nList-II\n(i) Brain present along with a number of ganglia and neural tissues\n(ii) Chemical integration through hormones\n(iii) Network of neurons\n(iv) More developed neural system\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): Hydra has a simple network of neurons.\nB-(i): Insects have a brain along with several ganglia and neural tissues.\nC-(iv): Vertebrates have a more developed neural system.\nD-(ii): The endocrine system provides chemical integration through hormones.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-028","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":28,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"The autonomic neural system is further classified into","image":"","options":["somatic and visceral neural systems","afferent and efferent neural systems","sympathetic and parasympathetic neural systems","central and peripheral neural systems"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The PNS is first split into the somatic and the autonomic divisions.\nThe autonomic neural system is then classified into the sympathetic and the parasympathetic neural systems.\nAfferent and efferent are types of nerve fibres, not subdivisions of the autonomic system.\nCentral and peripheral are the two main parts of the whole neural system.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-029","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":29,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Chemicals which are released at the synaptic junction are called","image":"","options":["Hormones","Neurotransmitters","Cerebrospinal fluid","Lymph"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"At a chemical synapse, an arriving impulse makes synaptic vesicles fuse with the pre-synaptic membrane.\nThey release chemicals called neurotransmitters (e.g., acetylcholine) into the synaptic cleft. These bind specific receptors on the post-synaptic membrane and open ion channels.\nHormones are tempting but wrong: they are secreted by endocrine glands into the blood, not into a synaptic cleft.\nCSF and lymph are body fluids, not signalling chemicals.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 21 (MCQ Q1)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-030","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":30,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Immediately after the rapid influx of $Na^+$ at the stimulated site of an axon, what is the charge distribution across the membrane at that site?","image":"","options":["Outer surface positive, inner surface negative","Both surfaces positive","Outer surface negative, inner surface positive","Both surfaces negative"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"At rest, the outer surface is positive and the inner surface is negative.\nThe influx of positively charged $Na^+$ reverses this polarity. The outer surface becomes negative and the inner surface becomes positive.\nThis reversal is called depolarisation.\nOption (1) describes the resting, polarised state, not the depolarised state.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-031","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":31,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Four round swellings called corpora quadrigemina are present in the human brain. They are located in the:","image":"","options":["Ventral portion of the pons","Dorsal surface of the cerebellum","Dorsal portion of the midbrain","Base of the thalamus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT describes the dorsal portion of the midbrain as consisting mainly of four round lobes, the corpora quadrigemina.\nThe midbrain lies between the thalamus/hypothalamus and the pons, and the cerebral aqueduct passes through it.\nThe base of the thalamus is the position of the hypothalamus, not the corpora quadrigemina.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-032","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":32,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The brain stem is made up of the midbrain, pons and medulla oblongata.\nStatement II: The cerebellum is one of the three regions forming the brain stem.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT names the midbrain, pons and medulla oblongata as the three regions of the brain stem.\nStatement I also fits the brain stem's role of connecting the brain with the spinal cord.\nStatement II is incorrect: the cerebellum is part of the hindbrain, but it is not a brain stem region.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-033","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":33,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly describes electrical synapses in the human neural system?","image":"","options":["They are the most common type of synapse","They are rare","They use neurotransmitters released from vesicles","They transmit impulses more slowly than chemical synapses"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that electrical synapses are rare in our system.\nAt an electrical synapse, current flows directly from one neuron to the next. No neurotransmitter is involved.\nTransmission across an electrical synapse is always faster than across a chemical synapse, so options (3) and (4) are wrong. Option (1) contradicts the fact that they are rare.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-034","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":34,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Neurons are excitable cells.\nReason (R): The membranes of neurons are in a polarised state.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Neurons can detect, receive and transmit stimuli, so they are excitable cells.\nR is true. At rest, the outer surface of the neuron membrane is positive and the inner surface is negative.\nNCERT states that neurons are excitable because their membranes are polarised. A stimulus can therefore reverse this polarity and produce an impulse.\nSo R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-035","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":35,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"A resting axon's sodium-potassium pump completes 150 cycles. Considering only the ions moved by the pump, what is the net number of positive charges transferred from the axoplasm to the outside fluid?","image":"","options":["450","300","150","750"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Each cycle moves 3 $Na^+$ out and 2 $K^+$ in.\nNet positive charge moved outward per cycle = 3 − 2 = 1.\nFor 150 cycles: 150 × 1 = 150 net positive charges moved outward.\nThis unequal exchange helps make the outer surface positive relative to the inner surface.\nOption (1) counts only the $Na^+$ moved out (450). Option (2) counts only the $K^+$ moved in (300).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-036","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":36,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: During physical exercise, the impulses from the CNS that increase the rate of heart beat are carried by the somatic neural system.\nStatement II: The somatic neural system relays impulses from the CNS to skeletal muscles, whereas involuntary organs are supplied by the autonomic neural system.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The heart is a vital involuntary organ, so its regulation from the CNS goes through the autonomic neural system.\nStatement I is therefore incorrect, because the somatic system does not carry these impulses.\nStatement II is correct. The somatic system supplies skeletal muscles, and the autonomic system supplies involuntary organs and smooth muscles.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-037","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":37,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"The neural system of Hydra is best described as:","image":"","options":["a brain along with a number of ganglia","a central brain and spinal cord","a network of neurons","a pair of ventral nerve cords with no neurons"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that neural organisation is very simple in lower invertebrates.\nIn Hydra, the neural system is just a network of neurons.\nA brain with ganglia (option 1) is the insect pattern, not Hydra's.\nA brain with a spinal cord (option 2) is the vertebrate CNS.\nOption (4) is wrong because every neural system is made of neurons.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-038","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":38,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Corpus callosum\n(B) Association areas\n(C) White matter\n(D) Neurosecretory cells\nList-II\n(I) Myelinated fibre tracts in the inner part of the cerebral hemispheres\n(II) Secretion of hypothalamic hormones\n(III) Intersensory associations, memory and communication\n(IV) Connection between the two cerebral hemispheres\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-IV: The corpus callosum is the fibre tract connecting the two hemispheres.\nB-III: Association areas are neither clearly sensory nor motor. They handle intersensory associations, memory and communication.\nC-I: Myelinated fibre tracts form the white matter in the inner part of the hemispheres.\nD-II: Neurosecretory cells of the hypothalamus secrete hypothalamic hormones.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-039","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":39,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Myelinated nerve fibres are found in spinal and cranial nerves, and the gaps between adjacent myelin sheaths are called nodes of Ranvier.\nStatement II: Non-myelinated nerve fibres have no association with Schwann cells.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Myelinated fibres occur in spinal and cranial nerves. Their myelin sheath is formed by Schwann cells, and the gaps between adjacent sheaths are nodes of Ranvier.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT says an unmyelinated fibre is enclosed by a Schwann cell, but that cell does not form a myelin sheath around the axon.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-040","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":40,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"A student describes a coronal slice of a cerebral hemisphere. Which one of the following descriptions is INCORRECT according to NCERT?","image":"","options":["The outer folded layer contains motor areas, sensory areas and association areas","The outer layer appears grey because neuron cell bodies are concentrated there","Myelinated fibre tracts lie deep to the cortex and appear white","The outer folded layer is white matter made of myelinated tracts, while grey matter lies in the interior"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the cerebral cortex has motor, sensory and association areas.\n(2) Correct: neuron cell bodies concentrated in the cortex give it a grey colour.\n(3) Correct: the inner part of the hemisphere is made of myelinated tracts and forms the white matter.\n(4) Incorrect: it reverses the arrangement. In the cerebrum, grey matter (the cortex) is on the outside and white matter is inside.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-041","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":41,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the human forebrain are correct?\n(i) The hypothalamus lies at the base of the thalamus.\n(ii) Amygdala and hippocampus are deep structures forming part of the limbic system.\n(iii) The cerebral cortex appears white because it is made of myelinated fibre tracts.\n(iv) The thalamus is a major coordinating centre for sensory and motor signalling.\n(v) Association areas of the cerebral cortex are clearly motor in function.\n(vi) The hypothalamus contains neurosecretory cells that secrete hypothalamic hormones.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the hypothalamus lies at the base of the thalamus.\n(ii) Correct: amygdala and hippocampus belong to the limbic system.\n(iii) Incorrect: the cortex is grey matter because of concentrated cell bodies. The myelinated tracts form the inner white matter.\n(iv) Correct: the thalamus coordinates sensory and motor signalling.\n(v) Incorrect: association areas are neither clearly sensory nor motor. They handle memory, communication and intersensory associations.\n(vi) Correct: the hypothalamus has neurosecretory cells.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-042","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":42,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Which event restores the resting potential at the site of excitation, so that the nerve fibre becomes responsive to further stimulation?","image":"","options":["Further influx of $Na^+$ into the axoplasm","Diffusion of $K^+$ to the outside of the membrane","Entry of negatively charged proteins into the axon","Diffusion of $K^+$ into the axoplasm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The rise in $Na^+$ permeability is very short-lived.\nIt is quickly followed by a rise in permeability to $K^+$.\nWithin a fraction of a second, $K^+$ diffuses outside the membrane, and this restores the resting potential (repolarisation).\nOption (4) has the direction reversed: $K^+$ moves out, not in.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-043","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":43,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are parts of the human forebrain?\nCerebrum, Thalamus, Corpora quadrigemina, Hypothalamus, Pons, Cerebellum, Medulla oblongata","image":"","options":["Two","Four","Three","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The forebrain consists of three parts: cerebrum, thalamus and hypothalamus.\nThe corpora quadrigemina are not included, because they are the four lobes of the dorsal midbrain.\nThe pons, cerebellum and medulla oblongata are not included, because together they make up the hindbrain.\nSo three of the listed structures belong to the forebrain.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-044","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":44,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are described in NCERT as components of the visceral nervous system?\nNerves, fibres, ganglia, plexuses, cerebral cortex, corpus callosum","image":"","options":["2","3","4","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT describes the visceral nervous system as the whole complex of nerves, fibres, ganglia and plexuses. Impulses travel through it between the CNS and the viscera.\nNerves, fibres, ganglia and plexuses are therefore components, which makes 4.\nThe cerebral cortex and the corpus callosum are parts of the cerebrum. They belong to the CNS, not this peripheral system.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-045","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":45,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II for a resting axon.\nList-I\n(a) $K^+$\n(b) $Na^+$\n(c) Negatively charged proteins\n(d) Sodium-potassium pump\nList-II\n(i) High concentration outside; membrane nearly impermeable to it\n(ii) High concentration inside; membrane comparatively permeable to it\n(iii) Confined to the axoplasm; membrane impermeable to it\n(iv) Maintains the ionic gradients by active transport","image":"","options":["a-(i), b-(ii), c-(iii), d-(iv)","a-(ii), b-(i), c-(iii), d-(iv)","a-(ii), b-(i), c-(iv), d-(iii)","a-(iii), b-(i), c-(ii), d-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) $K^+$ is high in the axoplasm, and the resting membrane is comparatively permeable to it. So a pairs with (ii).\n(b) $Na^+$ is high outside, and the resting membrane is nearly impermeable to it. So b pairs with (i).\n(c) Negatively charged proteins cannot cross the membrane, so they stay inside. So c pairs with (iii).\n(d) The pump maintains the gradients by active transport. So d pairs with (iv).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-046","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":46,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Corpora quadrigemina\n(b) Pons\n(c) Cerebellum\n(d) Medulla oblongata\nList-II\n(i) Contains centres controlling gastric secretions\n(ii) Four round swellings on the dorsal side of the midbrain\n(iii) Fibre tracts interconnecting different regions of the brain\n(iv) Integrates information from the semicircular canals of the ear\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Corpora quadrigemina are the four round lobes on the dorsal midbrain, so (a)-(ii).\n(b) The pons consists of fibre tracts that interconnect brain regions, so (b)-(iii).\n(c) The cerebellum integrates input from the semicircular canals and the auditory system, so (c)-(iv).\n(d) The medulla has centres for respiration, cardiovascular reflexes and gastric secretions, so (d)-(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-047","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":47,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, coordination in the body is best defined as the process through which:","image":"","options":["a single organ works independently to maintain its own internal environment","two or more organs interact and complement the functions of one another","hormones are released only during physical exercise","nerve impulses travel only from the brain to skeletal muscles"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines coordination as the process through which two or more organs interact and complement the functions of one another.\nThis coordination helps maintain homeostasis.\nOption (1) describes isolated action, which is the opposite of coordination.\nOptions (3) and (4) describe narrow, specific events, not the general process.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-048","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":48,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Unmyelinated nerve fibres are not associated with Schwann cells at all.\nReason (R): In an unmyelinated nerve fibre, the Schwann cell does not form a myelin sheath around the axon.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A is false. NCERT states that an unmyelinated nerve fibre is enclosed by a Schwann cell, so Schwann cells are present.\nR is true. In an unmyelinated fibre, the enclosing Schwann cell does not form a myelin sheath around the axon.\nSo the absence of myelin does not mean the absence of Schwann cells.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-049","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":49,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"What is the electrical potential difference across the plasma membrane at a site whose polarity has been reversed by a stimulus called?","image":"","options":["Resting potential","Synaptic potential","Equilibrium potential","Action potential"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"When a stimulus reverses the polarity at a site, that site is depolarised.\nThe potential difference across the membrane at such a site is the action potential. NCERT states that this is the nerve impulse.\nThe resting potential is the potential difference across the resting, polarised membrane, before any stimulus is applied.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-050","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":50,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Synaptic vesicles containing neurotransmitters are present in the:","image":"","options":["Dendrites near the cell body","Synaptic knobs at the axon terminals","Nodes of Ranvier","Post-synaptic membrane"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The distal end of an axon is branched. Each branch ends in a bulb-like synaptic knob.\nSynaptic knobs contain synaptic vesicles filled with neurotransmitters.\nDendrites carry impulses towards the cell body and do not store neurotransmitters for release. The post-synaptic membrane carries receptors, not vesicles.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-051","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":51,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"A person accidentally touches a hot pan and quickly pulls the hand back. The impulse travels from the skin to the spinal cord and then to the muscles of the arm, which are skeletal muscles. Which sequence correctly classifies this pathway?","image":"","options":["Efferent fibre (PNS) → CNS → afferent fibre of autonomic neural system","Afferent fibre (PNS) → CNS → efferent fibre of somatic neural system","Afferent fibre (CNS) → PNS → efferent fibre of autonomic neural system","Afferent fibre (PNS) → CNS → efferent fibre of parasympathetic neural system"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Skin to spinal cord: the impulse moves from a tissue to the CNS, so it travels on an afferent fibre of the PNS.\nThe spinal cord is part of the CNS, where the impulse is processed.\nSpinal cord to arm muscles: the impulse moves from the CNS to skeletal muscles. This is an efferent pathway of the somatic neural system.\nOptions (1), (3) and (4) wrongly involve autonomic or parasympathetic pathways, or place the afferent fibre in the CNS.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-052","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":52,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"A part of the forebrain contains centres that control body temperature and the urge for eating and drinking. This part is the:","image":"","options":["Thalamus","Cerebellum","Hypothalamus","Corpus callosum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The hypothalamus lies at the base of the thalamus.\nIt contains centres that control body temperature, hunger (eating) and thirst (drinking).\nThe thalamus is a coordinating centre for sensory and motor signals. It does not hold these centres.\nThe cerebellum belongs to the hindbrain.\nThe corpus callosum is only a fibre tract joining the two hemispheres.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-053","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":53,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Multipolar, bipolar and unipolar neurons each have exactly one axon.\n(ii) A unipolar neuron bears a single dendrite.\n(iii) Neurons of the retina of the eye are bipolar.\n(iv) Multipolar neurons are found in the cerebral cortex.\n(v) Nodes of Ranvier are present along unmyelinated nerve fibres.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: all three types have one axon. They differ only in the number of dendrites.\n(ii) Incorrect: a unipolar neuron has a cell body with one axon only and no dendrite.\n(iii) Correct: retinal neurons are bipolar.\n(iv) Correct: the cerebral cortex contains multipolar neurons.\n(v) Incorrect: nodes of Ranvier are gaps between myelin sheaths, so they exist only on myelinated fibres.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-054","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":54,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"A neuron that has one axon and only one dendrite is typically found in which of the following locations in the human body?","image":"","options":["Cerebral cortex","Retina of the eye","Embryonic stage tissues","Corpus callosum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT classifies neurons by the number of axons and dendrites.\nA neuron with one axon and one dendrite is bipolar, and NCERT gives the retina of the eye as its location.\nMultipolar neurons (one axon, two or more dendrites) occur in the cerebral cortex, so (1) is wrong.\nUnipolar neurons occur mainly in the embryonic stage, so (3) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-055","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":55,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following events at a single site of an axon in the correct sequence:\n(i) Rise in permeability to $K^+$\n(ii) Membrane becomes freely permeable to $Na^+$\n(iii) Reversal of polarity (inside positive)\n(iv) Rapid influx of $Na^+$\n(v) Resting potential restored\n(vi) $K^+$ diffuses outside the membrane","image":"","options":["(iv), (ii), (iii), (i), (vi), (v)","(ii), (iv), (iii), (i), (vi), (v)","(ii), (iv), (i), (iii), (vi), (v)","(ii), (iii), (iv), (vi), (i), (v)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The stimulus first makes the membrane freely permeable to $Na^+$ (ii).\nThis allows a rapid influx of $Na^+$ (iv), which reverses the polarity (iii).\nThe short-lived $Na^+$ permeability is followed by a rise in $K^+$ permeability (i).\n$K^+$ then diffuses out (vi), and the resting potential is restored (v).\nOption (1) fails because influx cannot occur before permeability increases.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-056","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":56,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"A patient has localised damage to the part of the brain stem that directly connects with the spinal cord. Which of the following functions is LEAST likely to be directly affected by this damage?","image":"","options":["Rhythm of breathing","Cardiovascular reflexes","Secretion of gastric juices","Integration of information from the semicircular canals"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The part of the brain stem connected to the spinal cord is the medulla oblongata.\nThe medulla contains centres for respiration, cardiovascular reflexes and gastric secretions. Options (1), (2) and (3) would therefore be directly affected.\nIntegrating information from the semicircular canals and the auditory system is a function of the cerebellum, which lies outside the brain stem.\nSo damage limited to the medulla is least likely to affect this function directly.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-057","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":57,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following structures are parts of the human hindbrain?\nPons, Cerebellum, Corpora quadrigemina, Medulla oblongata, Thalamus, Cerebral aqueduct","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Pons, cerebellum and medulla oblongata are hindbrain parts, which makes 3.\nCorpora quadrigemina are the four lobes on the dorsal midbrain.\nThe cerebral aqueduct is a canal passing through the midbrain.\nThe thalamus belongs to the forebrain.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-058","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":58,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about neuron structure are correct?\n(i) The axon is a long fibre whose distal end is branched.\n(ii) Dendrites are short, repeatedly branched fibres that lack Nissl's granules.\n(iii) Synaptic knobs possess synaptic vesicles containing neurotransmitters.\n(iv) An axon may carry impulses from the cell body to a neuro-muscular junction.\n(v) Each terminal branch of the axon ends as a synaptic knob.\n(vi) The cell body lacks the typical cell organelles.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the axon is long and its distal end is branched.\n(ii) Incorrect: dendrites do contain Nissl's granules.\n(iii) Correct: synaptic knobs hold vesicles with neurotransmitters.\n(iv) Correct: axons carry impulses to a synapse or a neuro-muscular junction.\n(v) Correct: each branch ends as a bulb-like synaptic knob.\n(vi) Incorrect: the cell body contains typical cell organelles along with Nissl's granules.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-059","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":59,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"In a neuron, Nissl's granules are found in which of the following?","image":"","options":["Cell body only","Axon and synaptic knobs","Cell body and dendrites","Synaptic knobs only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the cell body contains cytoplasm with typical organelles and granular bodies called Nissl's granules.\nIt also says the dendrites, the short branched fibres arising from the cell body, contain Nissl's granules.\n'Cell body only' is tempting but incomplete, because it ignores the dendrites.\nThe axon and synaptic knobs are not described as having Nissl's granules.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-060","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":60,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Afferent nerve fibres of the peripheral neural system carry regulatory impulses from the CNS to peripheral tissues and organs.\nStatement II: The autonomic neural system transmits impulses from the CNS to the involuntary organs and smooth muscles of the body.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Afferent fibres carry impulses from tissues and organs to the CNS. It is the efferent fibres that carry regulatory impulses from the CNS to the periphery.\nStatement II is correct. The autonomic system serves involuntary organs and smooth muscles. The somatic system serves skeletal muscles.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-061","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":61,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"The electrical potential difference across the plasma membrane at a depolarised site of an axon is termed:","image":"","options":["Resting potential","Synaptic potential","Action potential","Diffusion potential"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT calls the potential difference across the membrane at the depolarised site the action potential.\nThe action potential is what is termed the nerve impulse.\nResting potential is the potential difference across the resting (polarised) membrane, so (1) does not fit.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-062","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":62,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\nA. Medulla oblongata\nB. Hypothalamus\nC. Corpus callosum\nD. Pons\nList-II:\n(i) Fibre tracts that interconnect different regions of the brain\n(ii) Centres controlling respiration and gastric secretions\n(iii) Connects the left and right cerebral hemispheres\n(iv) Centres controlling body temperature and urge for drinking","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A-(ii): The medulla contains centres for respiration, cardiovascular reflexes and gastric secretions.\nB-(iv): The hypothalamus controls body temperature and the urge for eating and drinking.\nC-(iii): The corpus callosum is the tract of nerve fibres that joins the two cerebral hemispheres.\nD-(i): The pons consists of fibre tracts that interconnect different regions of the brain.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-063","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":63,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the neural membrane are correct?\n(i) The sodium-potassium pump transports 3 $Na^+$ outwards for every 2 $K^+$ brought into the cell.\n(ii) At rest, the axonal membrane is more permeable to $Na^+$ than to $K^+$.\n(iii) At rest, the axoplasm has a high concentration of $K^+$ and negatively charged proteins.\n(iv) The resting potential is restored at the site of excitation by a rise in $K^+$ permeability, with $K^+$ diffusing out.\n(v) At the site of an action potential, the outer surface of the membrane becomes positively charged.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the pump moves 3 $Na^+$ out for every 2 $K^+$ in.\n(ii) Incorrect: the resting membrane is more permeable to $K^+$ and nearly impermeable to $Na^+$.\n(iii) Correct: $K^+$ and negatively charged proteins are concentrated inside the axon.\n(iv) Correct: $K^+$ moving outward repolarises the membrane.\n(v) Incorrect: after $Na^+$ influx the outer surface becomes negative and the inner surface becomes positive.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-064","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":64,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The autonomic neural system regulates the contraction of smooth muscles in the wall of the gut.\nReason (R): The autonomic neural system relays impulses from the CNS to skeletal muscles.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The autonomic system transmits impulses from the CNS to involuntary organs and smooth muscles, and the gut wall contains smooth muscle.\nR is false. Impulses from the CNS to skeletal muscles are relayed by the somatic neural system, not the autonomic system.\nSince A is true and R is false, option (3) applies.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-065","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":65,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Multipolar neuron\nB. Bipolar neuron\nC. Unipolar neuron\nD. Myelinated nerve fibre\nList-II\n(i) Retina of the eye\n(ii) Usually in the embryonic stage\n(iii) Spinal and cranial nerves\n(iv) Cerebral cortex\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iii)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A: A multipolar neuron has one axon and two or more dendrites. It is found in the cerebral cortex, so A-(iv).\nB: A bipolar neuron has one axon and one dendrite. It is found in the retina, so B-(i).\nC: A unipolar neuron has a cell body with only one axon. It is found usually in the embryonic stage, so C-(ii).\nD: Myelinated fibres occur in spinal and cranial nerves, so D-(iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-066","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":66,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"A student studies three neurons under a microscope.\nNeuron X: the cell body bears one long fibre with a branched distal end and several short, repeatedly branched fibres.\nNeuron Y: the cell body bears one long fibre and only one short fibre.\nNeuron Z: the cell body bears a single long fibre and nothing else.\nWhich option correctly identifies the neurons and their usual locations?","image":"","options":["X – bipolar, retina; Y – multipolar, cerebral cortex; Z – unipolar, embryonic stage","X – multipolar, cerebral cortex; Y – unipolar, embryonic stage; Z – bipolar, retina","X – multipolar, cerebral cortex; Y – bipolar, retina; Z – unipolar, embryonic stage","X – unipolar, embryonic stage; Y – bipolar, retina; Z – multipolar, cerebral cortex"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The long fibre with a branched distal end is the axon. The short, repeatedly branched fibres are dendrites.\nX has one axon and many dendrites, so it is multipolar, found in the cerebral cortex.\nY has one axon and one dendrite, so it is bipolar, found in the retina.\nZ has one axon only, so it is unipolar, found usually in the embryonic stage.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-067","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":67,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Within a fraction of a second after an impulse passes a site on an axon, the fibre at that site becomes responsive to further stimulation.\nReason (R): The short-lived rise in $Na^+$ permeability is quickly followed by a rise in $K^+$ permeability, and $K^+$ diffuses out to restore the resting potential.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that within a fraction of a second the fibre becomes responsive again.\nR is true: the stimulus-induced $Na^+$ permeability is extremely short-lived and is followed by a rise in $K^+$ permeability. $K^+$ then diffuses outside.\nThis $K^+$ efflux restores the resting potential, and that is exactly why the fibre can be stimulated again. So R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-068","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":68,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"The following events occur at one site on an axon during generation of a nerve impulse:\n(a) Permeability to $K^+$ rises and $K^+$ diffuses out of the axon\n(b) The membrane becomes freely permeable to $Na^+$\n(c) Rapid influx of $Na^+$ makes the inner surface positive\n(d) A stimulus is applied to the polarised membrane\n(e) The resting potential is restored and the site can respond to a fresh stimulus\nWhich is the correct sequence?","image":"","options":["(b), (d), (c), (e), (a)","(d), (c), (b), (a), (e)","(d), (b), (c), (a), (e)","(d), (b), (a), (c), (e)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"First, a stimulus acts on the polarised membrane (d).\nThe membrane then becomes freely permeable to $Na^+$ (b), and $Na^+$ rushes in, reversing the polarity (c).\nThe $Na^+$ permeability quickly falls and $K^+$ permeability rises, so $K^+$ diffuses out (a).\nThis restores the resting potential, and the site becomes responsive again (e).\nOption (2) wrongly places $Na^+$ influx before the rise in $Na^+$ permeability.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-069","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":69,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Which organ is described in NCERT as the 'command and control system' of the body and as the site for processing vision, hearing, speech, memory, intelligence, emotions and thoughts?","image":"","options":["Spinal cord","Brain","Autonomic neural system","Peripheral neural system"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The brain is the central information processing organ of the body and acts as its 'command and control system'.\nIt is also the site where vision, hearing, speech, memory, intelligence, emotions and thoughts are processed.\nThe spinal cord is part of the CNS, but NCERT does not give it these higher functions.\nThe PNS and the autonomic neural system only carry impulses to and from the CNS; they do not process this information.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-070","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":70,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"The cerebral cortex is called grey matter. What gives it this greyish appearance?","image":"","options":["Myelin sheaths around the fibre tracts","Cerebrospinal fluid in the folds","Concentration of neuron cell bodies","Blood capillaries of the pia mater"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that neuron cell bodies are concentrated in the cerebral cortex, and this gives it a greyish colour.\nMyelin sheaths cause the opposite effect. They give the inner layer of the hemispheres an opaque white appearance, which is why that layer is called white matter.\nThe other two options are not given as the reason for the grey colour.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-071","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":71,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Which tract of nerve fibres connects the left and right cerebral hemispheres of the human brain?","image":"","options":["Cerebral aqueduct","Corpus callosum","Corpora quadrigemina","Pons"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A deep longitudinal cleft divides the cerebrum into left and right cerebral hemispheres.\nThe corpus callosum is the tract of nerve fibres that joins these two hemispheres.\nThe cerebral aqueduct is a canal passing through the midbrain. The corpora quadrigemina are four lobes in the dorsal midbrain. The pons is a hindbrain region whose fibre tracts link different brain regions, not the two hemispheres.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-072","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":72,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) A deep longitudinal cleft divides the cerebrum into left and right cerebral hemispheres.\n(ii) The cerebral cortex is a smooth layer without folds.\n(iii) The thalamus wraps around the cerebrum.\n(iv) The hypothalamus contains neurosecretory cells that secrete hormones.\n(v) Association areas of the cortex are responsible for memory and communication.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: a deep cleft divides the cerebrum into two hemispheres.\n(ii) Incorrect: the cortex is thrown into prominent folds.\n(iii) Incorrect: the relation is the other way round, since the cerebrum wraps around the thalamus.\n(iv) Correct: neurosecretory cells of the hypothalamus secrete hypothalamic hormones.\n(v) Correct: association areas handle intersensory associations, memory and communication.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-073","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":73,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Which feature correctly describes the type of coordination provided by the neural system?","image":"","options":["Chemical integration through hormones","An organised network of point-to-point connections for quick coordination","Slow, long-lasting integration carried by blood","Coordination limited to involuntary organs only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the neural system provides an organised network of point-to-point connections for quick coordination.\nChemical integration through hormones (option 1) is the role of the endocrine system.\nOption (3) does not fit the neural system, which acts quickly through direct connections.\nOption (4) is wrong because the neural system also controls voluntary activities.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-074","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":74,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"The surface of the cerebellum is highly convoluted. According to NCERT, this convolution","image":"","options":["provides additional space for many more neurons","allows the cerebral aqueduct to pass through it","connects the two cerebral hemispheres","forms the four lobes of the corpora quadrigemina"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the cerebellum has a very convoluted surface to provide additional space for many more neurons.\nThe cerebral aqueduct passes through the midbrain, not the cerebellum.\nThe corpus callosum connects the two cerebral hemispheres.\nThe corpora quadrigemina are swellings on the dorsal midbrain.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-075","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":75,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Corpus callosum\n(b) Medulla oblongata\n(c) Hypothalamus\n(d) Pons\nList-II\n(i) Fibre tracts that interconnect different regions of the brain\n(ii) Centres controlling respiration and cardiovascular reflexes\n(iii) Tract of nerve fibres connecting the two cerebral hemispheres\n(iv) Centres controlling body temperature and the urge to drink\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Corpus callosum connects the left and right cerebral hemispheres, so (iii).\n(b) The medulla has centres for respiration, cardiovascular reflexes and gastric secretions, so (ii).\n(c) The hypothalamus controls body temperature and the urge for eating and drinking, so (iv).\n(d) The pons consists of fibre tracts that interconnect brain regions, so (i).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-076","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":76,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Skull\n(B) Dura mater\n(C) Arachnoid\n(D) Pia mater\nList-II\n(i) Very thin middle covering\n(ii) Innermost covering in contact with brain tissue\n(iii) Bony protective case of the brain\n(iv) Outer cranial meninx","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-iv, C-i, D-ii","A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i","A-iii, B-i, C-iv, D-ii","A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Skull – (iii): the bony case that protects the brain.\n(B) Dura mater – (iv): the outer cranial meninx.\n(C) Arachnoid – (i): the very thin middle layer.\n(D) Pia mater – (ii): the inner layer that contacts the brain tissue.\nOption (4) wrongly swaps the arachnoid and pia mater.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-077","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":77,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Bipolar neurons, which have one axon and one dendrite, are found in the:","image":"","options":["Cerebral cortex","Retina of the eye","Embryonic stage only","Corpus callosum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT classifies neurons by the number of axons and dendrites they have.\nBipolar neurons have one axon and one dendrite and are found in the retina of the eye.\nThe cerebral cortex is the tempting wrong option, but it contains multipolar neurons.\nUnipolar neurons are found usually in the embryonic stage.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-078","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":78,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Site A of an axon has just been depolarised, and site B lies immediately ahead of it. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Direction of current on the inner surface of the membrane\n(B) Direction of current on the outer surface of the membrane\n(C) Ion whose rapid influx depolarises site A\n(D) Process that restores the resting potential at site A\nList-II\n(i) $Na^+$\n(ii) From site B to site A\n(iii) $K^+$ diffusing out of the axon\n(iv) From site A to site B","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A)-(iv): the inner surface is positive at A and negative at B, so current flows from A to B.\n(B)-(ii): the outer surface is negative at A and positive at B, so current flows from B to A, completing the circuit.\n(C)-(i): rapid $Na^+$ influx depolarises site A.\n(D)-(iii): $K^+$ diffusing outward restores the resting potential.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-079","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":79,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, the myelin sheath around a myelinated axon is formed by:","image":"","options":["Schwann cells","Nissl's granules","Synaptic vesicles","Dendrites of adjacent neurons"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that myelinated nerve fibres are enveloped by Schwann cells, which form the myelin sheath.\nNissl's granules are granular bodies in the cell body and dendrites.\nSynaptic vesicles store neurotransmitters in the synaptic knobs.\nDendrites carry impulses towards the cell body and do not wrap other axons.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-080","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":80,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Central neural system\n(B) Efferent fibres\n(C) Somatic neural system\n(D) Visceral nervous system\nList-II\n(i) Relays impulses from the CNS to skeletal muscles\n(ii) Brain and spinal cord; site of information processing and control\n(iii) Carry regulatory impulses from the CNS to peripheral tissues/organs\n(iv) Nerves, fibres, ganglia and plexuses carrying impulses between the CNS and the viscera in both directions","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(ii): The CNS consists of the brain and spinal cord and is the site of information processing and control.\nB-(iii): Efferent fibres carry regulatory impulses from the CNS to peripheral tissues and organs.\nC-(i): The somatic neural system relays impulses from the CNS to skeletal muscles.\nD-(iv): The visceral nervous system includes nerves, fibres, ganglia and plexuses. It carries impulses both to and from the viscera.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-081","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":81,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following structures are regions of the human brain stem?\nMidbrain, Cerebellum, Pons, Thalamus, Medulla oblongata, Hypothalamus","image":"","options":["4","3","2","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that three regions make up the brain stem: midbrain, pons and medulla oblongata.\nThe cerebellum is part of the hindbrain, but it is not counted in the brain stem.\nThe thalamus and hypothalamus belong to the forebrain.\nSo the count is 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-082","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":82,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"The organ of corti is a structure present in","image":"","options":["External ear","Middle ear","Semi circular canal","Cochlea"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key NCERT fact: the organ of Corti sits on the basilar membrane inside the cochlea of the internal ear. It is the receptor for hearing.\nIts hair cells are arranged in rows. The tectorial membrane lies over them. Sound vibrations bend the hair cells, and this produces nerve impulses in the auditory nerve.\nWhy the other options fail:\n- The external and middle ears only collect and conduct sound. They contain no receptor organ.\n- The semicircular canals belong to the vestibular apparatus. Their receptor is the crista ampullaris, which is used for balance.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 21 (MCQ Q11)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-083","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":83,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"In the human brain, the left and right cerebral hemispheres are connected to each other by a tract of nerve fibres called the:","image":"","options":["Corpus callosum","Corpora quadrigemina","Cerebral aqueduct","Pons"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A deep cleft divides the cerebrum lengthwise into left and right cerebral hemispheres.\nThese two halves are joined by a tract of nerve fibres called the corpus callosum.\nCorpora quadrigemina are four round lobes on the dorsal side of the midbrain.\nThe cerebral aqueduct is a canal that passes through the midbrain.\nPons is in the hindbrain. Its fibre tracts link different brain regions, not the two hemispheres.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-084","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":84,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"A neuron with one axon and one dendrite arising from its cell body would most likely be found in which of the following locations in the human body?","image":"","options":["Cerebral cortex","Embryonic neural tissue","Retina of the eye","Corpus callosum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A neuron with one axon and one dendrite is a bipolar neuron.\nNCERT states that bipolar neurons are found in the retina of the eye.\nThe cerebral cortex has multipolar neurons, which have one axon and two or more dendrites.\nUnipolar neurons, with a cell body and one axon only, are found usually in the embryonic stage.\nThe corpus callosum is a tract of nerve fibres, not a site of bipolar neurons.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-085","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":85,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Multipolar neuron\n(B) Bipolar neuron\n(C) Unipolar neuron\n(D) Nodes of Ranvier\nList-II\n(i) Found usually in the embryonic stage\n(ii) Gaps between two adjacent myelin sheaths\n(iii) Found in the cerebral cortex\n(iv) Found in the retina of the eye\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A–(iii): Multipolar neurons have one axon and two or more dendrites and are found in the cerebral cortex.\nB–(iv): Bipolar neurons have one axon and one dendrite and are found in the retina.\nC–(i): Unipolar neurons have a cell body with one axon only and are found usually in the embryonic stage.\nD–(ii): Nodes of Ranvier are the gaps between two adjacent myelin sheaths.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-086","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":86,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Impulse transmission across an electrical synapse is faster than across a chemical synapse.\nReason (R): At an electrical synapse, electrical current flows directly from the pre-synaptic neuron into the post-synaptic neuron.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that transmission across an electrical synapse is always faster than across a chemical synapse.\nR is true. The membranes at an electrical synapse are in very close proximity, so current passes directly from one neuron to the other.\nR explains A. Because current passes directly, there is no delay from vesicle release, diffusion across a cleft or receptor binding.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-087","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":87,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"The function of our visceral organs is controlled by","image":"","options":["Sympathetic and somatic neural system","Sympathetic and parasympathetic neural system","Central and somatic nervous system","None of the above"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Visceral organs (heart, gut, glands, smooth muscles) work involuntarily. They are controlled by the autonomic neural system, a part of the PNS.\nThe autonomic system has two divisions, sympathetic and parasympathetic. These usually act antagonistically, for example speeding up and slowing down the heart.\nThe somatic neural system is wrong because it carries impulses from the CNS to skeletal (voluntary) muscles.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 21 (MCQ Q4)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-088","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":88,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the generation of an action potential are correct?\n(i) The stimulus-induced rise in $Na^+$ permeability is long-lasting.\n(ii) At the stimulated site, the inner surface of the membrane becomes positively charged.\n(iii) The action potential is in fact termed the nerve impulse.\n(iv) The impulse generated at site A is conducted to site B by repetition of the same sequence along the axon.","image":"","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: the rise in $Na^+$ permeability is extremely short-lived.\n(ii) Correct: the $Na^+$ influx makes the inside positive.\n(iii) Correct: NCERT states that the action potential is termed the nerve impulse.\n(iv) Correct: the sequence repeats along the length of the axon, conducting the impulse.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-089","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":89,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the resting state of a neuron are correct?\n(i) The concentration gradients of $Na^+$ and $K^+$ across the membrane are maintained by active transport.\n(ii) The outer surface of the axonal membrane is negatively charged.\n(iii) The axoplasm contains a low concentration of $Na^+$.\n(iv) Neurons are excitable because their membranes are in a polarised state.\n(v) The axonal membrane at rest is freely permeable to $Na^+$.","image":"","options":["Two","Four","Five","Three"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the sodium-potassium pump maintains the gradients by active transport.\n(ii) Incorrect: at rest, the outer surface is positive.\n(iii) Correct: $Na^+$ is low inside the axon.\n(iv) Correct: NCERT says neurons are excitable because their membranes are polarised.\n(v) Incorrect: at rest the membrane is nearly impermeable to $Na^+$. It becomes freely permeable only after a stimulus.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-090","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":90,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Inside the skull, which cranial meninx is in direct contact with the brain tissue?","image":"","options":["Dura mater","Arachnoid","Pia mater","Cerebral cortex"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The brain is covered by three cranial meninges: the outer dura mater, the very thin middle arachnoid and the inner pia mater.\nThe pia mater is the innermost layer, so it touches the brain tissue.\nDura mater is the outermost layer, so it cannot be in contact with brain tissue.\nThe cerebral cortex is part of the brain itself, not a meninx.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-091","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":91,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Potential difference across resting membrane is negative. This is due to differential distribution of the following ions","image":"","options":["Na$^+$ and K$^+$","CO$_3^{2-}$ and Cl$^-$","Ca$^{++}$ and Mg$^{++}$","Ca$^{++}$ and Cl$^-$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"At rest, the axoplasm has high K$^+$ and low Na$^+$. The extracellular fluid has high Na$^+$ and low K$^+$.\nThe resting membrane is more permeable to K$^+$ than to Na$^+$. The sodium-potassium pump also sends out 3 Na$^+$ for every 2 K$^+$ it brings in.\nSo the outer surface becomes positive and the inner surface negative. This state is the polarised membrane.\nCa$^{++}$, Mg$^{++}$ and Cl$^-$ are not the ions NCERT names for this potential.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 21 (MCQ Q2)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-092","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":92,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Hypothalamus\nB. Medulla oblongata\nC. Corpus callosum\nD. Limbic system\nList-II\n(i) Tract of nerve fibres connecting the two cerebral hemispheres\n(ii) Expression of emotional reactions such as rage and fear\n(iii) Centres controlling body temperature and the urge for drinking\n(iv) Centres controlling respiration and gastric secretions\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A: The hypothalamus has centres for body temperature, eating and drinking, so A-(iii).\nB: The medulla controls respiration, cardiovascular reflexes and gastric secretions, so B-(iv).\nC: The corpus callosum is the fibre tract joining the two hemispheres, so C-(i).\nD: The limbic system, together with the hypothalamus, regulates emotional reactions, so D-(ii).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-093","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":93,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"The centres that control respiration, cardiovascular reflexes and gastric secretions are located in the","image":"","options":["pons","cerebellum","medulla oblongata","corpora quadrigemina"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the medulla contains centres that control respiration, cardiovascular reflexes and gastric secretions.\nThe pons is the most tempting wrong option. It is also in the hindbrain, but NCERT describes it only as fibre tracts that interconnect brain regions.\nThe cerebellum integrates inputs from the semicircular canals and the auditory system.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-094","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":94,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): During conduction of an impulse, current flows on the outer surface of the axonal membrane from site B (ahead) to site A (stimulated).\nReason (R): At that moment, the outer surface at site B is still positively charged, while the outer surface at site A has become negatively charged.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that on the outer surface, current flows from site B to site A, completing the circuit.\nR is true. Site B is still polarised (positive outside), while depolarised site A is negative outside.\nCurrent flows from the positive region to the negative region, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-095","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":95,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Coordinating physical exercise requires the participation of organs other than skeletal muscles, such as the lungs, heart and blood vessels.\nReason (R): Increased muscular activity raises energy demand, which requires more oxygen, and this in turn needs a higher rate of respiration, heart beat and blood flow.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that muscles, lungs, heart, blood vessels, kidney and other organs are coordinated during exercise.\nR is true. It traces the chain: more energy demand leads to more oxygen needed, which leads to faster respiration, faster heart beat and more blood flow.\nR explains why non-muscle organs must take part, so it correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-096","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":96,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Impulse transmission across an electrical synapse is faster than across a chemical synapse.\nReason (R): Electrical synapses are rare in the human neural system.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. At electrical synapses the pre- and post-synaptic membranes are in very close proximity, so current flows directly between them and transmission is faster.\nR is true. NCERT states that electrical synapses are rare in our system.\nHowever, rarity has no bearing on speed. The real reason for the speed is direct current flow without any neurotransmitter step, so R does not explain A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-097","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":97,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about the human brain is correct?","image":"","options":["The thalamus lies at the base of the hypothalamus and contains neurosecretory cells","The midbrain lies between the pons and the medulla oblongata","The corpora quadrigemina are four round swellings on the ventral side of the midbrain","The brain stem consists of the midbrain, pons and medulla oblongata, and the cerebral aqueduct passes through the midbrain"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Wrong: the positions are reversed. The hypothalamus lies at the base of the thalamus, and the neurosecretory cells are in the hypothalamus.\n(2) Wrong: the midbrain lies between the thalamus/hypothalamus and the pons.\n(3) Wrong: the corpora quadrigemina form the dorsal portion of the midbrain, not the ventral side.\n(4) Correct: the brain stem is made of the midbrain, pons and medulla, and the cerebral aqueduct runs through the midbrain.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-098","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":98,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Dendrites of a neuron transmit nerve impulses","image":"","options":["away from the cell body towards the synapse","towards the cell body","directly to the neuromuscular junction","from the synaptic knob to the axon"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Dendrites are short, repeatedly branched fibres that project from the cell body.\nNCERT states that these fibres transmit impulses towards the cell body.\nCarrying impulses away from the cell body to a synapse or neuromuscular junction is the job of the axon, so options (1) and (3) describe the axon instead.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-099","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":99,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"An axon is stimulated at site A, which becomes depolarised. Site B lies immediately ahead and is still in the resting state. Which option correctly describes the local current flow that generates an action potential at B?","image":"","options":["Inner surface: from B to A; outer surface: from A to B","Inner surface: from A to B; outer surface: from B to A","On both surfaces: from A to B","On both surfaces: from B to A"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"At A the polarity is reversed: the inside is positive and the outside is negative.\nB is still polarised: the inside is negative and the outside is positive.\nCurrent flows from positive to negative. On the inner surface it therefore flows from A (+) to B (−).\nOn the outer surface it flows from B (+) to A (−), which completes the circuit.\nThis local current reverses the polarity at B, so an action potential is generated there.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-100","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":100,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Some events of impulse transmission across a chemical synapse are listed below.\n(i) Neurotransmitters bind to specific receptors on the post-synaptic membrane\n(ii) An action potential arrives at the axon terminal\n(iii) Synaptic vesicles fuse with the plasma membrane and release neurotransmitters into the cleft\n(iv) Ion channels open and ions enter, generating a new potential in the post-synaptic neuron\n(v) Synaptic vesicles move towards the pre-synaptic membrane\nWhich option gives the correct sequence?","image":"","options":["(ii) → (iii) → (v) → (i) → (iv)","(ii) → (v) → (iii) → (i) → (iv)","(v) → (ii) → (iii) → (iv) → (i)","(ii) → (v) → (i) → (iii) → (iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The process begins when the action potential arrives at the axon terminal (ii). \nThis stimulates the synaptic vesicles to move towards the membrane (v). \nThe vesicles then fuse with the membrane and release neurotransmitters into the cleft (iii). \nThe neurotransmitters bind to their receptors on the post-synaptic membrane (i). \nThis binding opens ion channels, and the entering ions generate a new potential (iv). \nOption (1) is wrong because vesicles must move to the membrane before they can fuse with it. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-101","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":101,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"When a stimulus is applied at site A on the polarised membrane of an axon, what change occurs first at that site?","image":"","options":["The membrane becomes freely permeable to $K^+$","The membrane becomes freely permeable to $Na^+$","The membrane becomes permeable to negatively charged proteins","The sodium-potassium pump stops working"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT: a stimulus at site A makes the membrane at that site freely permeable to $Na^+$.\nThis causes a rapid influx of $Na^+$, which leads to depolarisation.\nThe rise in $K^+$ permeability comes later, during restoration of the resting potential, so (1) is out of sequence.\nThe membrane stays impermeable to the negatively charged proteins of the axoplasm.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-102","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":102,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: During physical exercise, the increased supply of oxygen requires an increase in the rate of respiration, heart beat and blood flow through blood vessels.\nStatement II: When physical exercise stops, the activities of nerves, lungs, heart and kidney return to normal abruptly, at the very instant exercise ends.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. More muscular activity raises the energy demand and the need for oxygen. This calls for faster respiration, a faster heart beat and more blood flow.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT says these activities return to normal gradually, not abruptly.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-103","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":103,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Every synapse has a synaptic cleft separating the pre-synaptic and post-synaptic membranes.\nStatement II: The new potential generated in a post-synaptic neuron at a chemical synapse may be either excitatory or inhibitory.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. NCERT says the two membranes may or may not be separated by a synaptic cleft. At electrical synapses, the membranes are in very close proximity.\nStatement II is correct. Ion entry after neurotransmitter binding produces a new potential, which may be excitatory or inhibitory.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-104","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":104,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about synapses are correct?\n(i) At electrical synapses, the pre-synaptic and post-synaptic membranes lie in very close proximity.\n(ii) Transmission across an electrical synapse is very similar to impulse conduction along a single axon.\n(iii) Neurotransmitters reach the synaptic cleft when synaptic vesicles fuse with the plasma membrane of the axon terminal.\n(iv) Binding of neurotransmitters to their receptors closes ion channels on the post-synaptic membrane.\n(v) Chemical synapses are rare in the human neural system.","image":"","options":["Two","Four","Three","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this close proximity is a defining feature of electrical synapses.\n(ii) Correct: NCERT compares transmission across an electrical synapse to conduction along a single axon.\n(iii) Correct: vesicles fuse with the plasma membrane and release their contents into the cleft.\n(iv) Incorrect: binding opens ion channels, allowing ions to enter.\n(v) Incorrect: it is electrical synapses that are rare.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-105","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":105,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following together constitute the central neural system (CNS) in humans?","image":"","options":["Brain and cranial nerves","Brain and spinal cord","Spinal cord and spinal nerves","Brain, spinal cord and all associated nerves"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The human neural system has two parts, the CNS and the PNS.\nThe CNS consists of the brain and the spinal cord. It is the site of information processing and control.\nCranial nerves and spinal nerves are associated with the CNS, but they belong to the PNS.\nOption (4) is wrong because it adds the nerves to the CNS.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-106","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":106,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, the human brain can be divided into which three major parts?","image":"","options":["Cerebrum, cerebellum and medulla","Brain stem, cerebrum and spinal cord","Forebrain, midbrain and hindbrain","Thalamus, hypothalamus and pons"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT divides the brain into three major parts: the forebrain, the midbrain and the hindbrain.\nCerebrum, cerebellum, medulla, thalamus, hypothalamus and pons are structures found within these parts, not the three main divisions.\nThe spinal cord belongs to the CNS but is not a part of the brain.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-107","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":107,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"The cerebral cortex is called grey matter because:","image":"","options":["It is made mainly of myelinated fibre tracts","Neuron cell bodies are concentrated in it","It contains the cerebrospinal fluid-filled cerebral aqueduct","It is covered by the pia mater"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The cerebral cortex is the folded layer of cells covering each cerebral hemisphere.\nIts greyish colour comes from the neuron cell bodies concentrated there.\nOption (1) describes white matter. Myelin-covered fibre tracts look opaque white and form the inner part of the hemisphere.\nThe pia mater is a meningeal layer and does not explain the grey colour.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-108","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":108,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the resting neural membrane are correct?\n(i) The ion channels on the neural membrane are selectively permeable to different ions.\n(ii) The axoplasm has a lower $Na^+$ concentration than the fluid outside the axon.\n(iii) Each cycle of the sodium-potassium pump moves one more positive charge out of the cell than it brings in.\n(iv) The resting state exists because the membrane is freely permeable to $Na^+$.\n(v) Negatively charged proteins leak out of the axon at rest, making the outer surface negative.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. Different ion channels are selectively permeable to different ions.\n(ii) Correct. $Na^+$ is low inside the axon and high outside.\n(iii) Correct. The pump moves 3 $Na^+$ out and 2 $K^+$ in, so there is a net outward movement of one positive charge per cycle.\n(iv) Incorrect. The resting membrane is nearly impermeable to $Na^+$. Free $Na^+$ permeability occurs only after a stimulus.\n(v) Incorrect. The membrane is impermeable to these proteins, and the outer surface is positive.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-109","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":109,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Dendrites\n(b) Synaptic knob\n(c) Nodes of Ranvier\n(d) Schwann cells\nList-II\n(i) Gaps between two adjacent myelin sheaths\n(ii) Form the myelin sheath around the axon\n(iii) Short, repeatedly branched fibres containing Nissl's granules\n(iv) Bulb-like ending containing vesicles of neurotransmitters\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(a) Dendrites are short branched fibres with Nissl's granules that carry impulses toward the cell body, so (iii).\n(b) Each axon branch ends in a synaptic knob holding synaptic vesicles with neurotransmitters, so (iv).\n(c) Nodes of Ranvier are the gaps between adjacent myelin sheaths, so (i).\n(d) Schwann cells form the myelin sheath on myelinated fibres, so (ii).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-110","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":110,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"The synaptic vesicles present in synaptic knobs contain","image":"","options":["Nissl's granules","myelin","sodium-potassium pumps","neurotransmitters"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Each branch at the distal end of the axon ends as a synaptic knob.\nThe synaptic knob has synaptic vesicles that contain chemicals called neurotransmitters.\nNissl's granules are found in the cell body and dendrites, not inside vesicles.\nMyelin is the sheath formed around some axons by Schwann cells.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-111","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":111,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Afferent nerve fibres of the peripheral neural system carry impulses","image":"","options":["from the CNS to skeletal muscles","from the CNS to smooth muscles only","from tissues/organs to the CNS","from one cerebral hemisphere to the other"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"PNS fibres are of two types, afferent and efferent.\nAfferent fibres carry impulses from tissues and organs towards the CNS.\nEfferent fibres carry regulatory impulses from the CNS to peripheral tissues and organs. Options (1) and (2) therefore describe efferent pathways.\nThe link between the two cerebral hemispheres is the corpus callosum, which is not a PNS fibre type.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-112","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":112,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the structure of a neuron are correct?\n(i) Dendrites are short fibres that branch repeatedly.\n(ii) The axon is a long fibre whose proximal end (near the cell body) is branched into synaptic knobs.\n(iii) An axon may carry impulses to a neuro-muscular junction.\n(iv) Synaptic vesicles are present in the synaptic knobs.\n(v) Nissl's granules are absent from dendrites.","image":"","options":["Two","Four","Five","Three"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: dendrites are short, repeatedly branched fibres.\n(ii) Incorrect: it is the distal end of the axon that is branched and ends in synaptic knobs.\n(iii) Correct: axons carry impulses to a synapse or to a neuro-muscular junction.\n(iv) Correct: synaptic knobs contain synaptic vesicles.\n(v) Incorrect: dendrites also contain Nissl's granules.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-113","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":113,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the human neural system is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The CNS is the site of information processing and control.","The PNS comprises all the nerves of the body associated with the brain and spinal cord.","The visceral nervous system is a part of the CNS that directly supplies the viscera.","The autonomic neural system transmits impulses from the CNS to smooth muscles of the body."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the CNS processes information and exerts control.\n(2) Correct: this is how NCERT defines the PNS.\n(3) Incorrect: the visceral nervous system is a part of the peripheral nervous system, not the CNS.\n(4) Correct: the autonomic system supplies involuntary organs and smooth muscles.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-114","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":114,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) A multipolar neuron has one axon and two or more dendrites.\n(ii) A unipolar neuron has a cell body bearing one axon and one dendrite.\n(iii) Myelinated nerve fibres are found in spinal and cranial nerves.\n(iv) Unmyelinated nerve fibres are commonly found in the autonomous and somatic neural systems.\n(v) Nodes of Ranvier are the regions where the myelin sheath is thickest.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NCERT describes a multipolar neuron as having one axon and two or more dendrites.\n(ii) Incorrect: a unipolar neuron has a cell body with one axon only. One axon plus one dendrite describes a bipolar neuron.\n(iii) Correct: myelinated fibres occur in spinal and cranial nerves.\n(iv) Correct: unmyelinated fibres are common in the autonomous and somatic neural systems.\n(v) Incorrect: nodes of Ranvier are gaps between adjacent myelin sheaths, so myelin is absent there.\nCorrect statements are (i), (iii) and (iv), which makes 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-115","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":115,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: At the instant site A of an axon is depolarised, the membrane at site B immediately ahead still carries a positive charge on its outer surface and a negative charge on its inner surface.\nStatement II: The resting potential at site A is restored by the inward movement of $K^+$ into the axoplasm.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Site B has not yet been reached by the impulse, so it remains polarised: outer surface positive, inner surface negative. This charge difference is what drives local current flow between A and B.\nStatement II is incorrect. After the brief $Na^+$ influx, $K^+$ diffuses outside the membrane, not inward. This outward diffusion restores the resting potential.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-116","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":116,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Which sequence correctly shows the chain of events during physical exercise, as described in NCERT?","image":"","options":["Increased respiration rate → increased muscular activity → increased energy demand → increased oxygen supply","Increased muscular activity → increased energy demand → increased oxygen supply → increased rate of respiration, heart beat and blood flow","Increased oxygen supply → decreased heart beat → increased energy demand → increased muscular activity","Increased blood flow → decreased energy demand → increased muscular activity → decreased respiration"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Exercise increases muscular activity, which raises the energy demand.\nTo meet this demand, the supply of oxygen is increased.\nThe higher oxygen supply in turn needs a faster rate of respiration, a faster heart beat and more blood flow.\nOptions (3) and (4) include decreases, which contradict the NCERT example.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-117","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":117,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Corpus callosum\n(B) Hypothalamus\n(C) Thalamus\n(D) Association areas\nList-II\n(I) Intersensory associations, memory and communication\n(II) Tract of nerve fibres linking the two cerebral hemispheres\n(III) Neurosecretory cells secreting hypothalamic hormones\n(IV) Major coordinating centre for sensory and motor signalling","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A-II: the corpus callosum is the fibre tract that joins the left and right hemispheres.\nB-III: the hypothalamus has groups of neurosecretory cells that secrete hypothalamic hormones.\nC-IV: the thalamus is a major coordinating centre for sensory and motor signalling.\nD-I: association areas handle intersensory associations, memory and communication.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-118","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":118,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are parts of the human forebrain?\nCerebrum, thalamus, corpora quadrigemina, hypothalamus, pons, corpus callosum, medulla oblongata, cerebral aqueduct","image":"","options":["3","5","4","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Forebrain: the cerebrum, thalamus and hypothalamus. The corpus callosum is also included because it connects the cerebral hemispheres. That makes 4.\nMidbrain: the corpora quadrigemina (dorsal lobes) and the cerebral aqueduct (the canal through it).\nHindbrain: the pons and medulla oblongata.\nSo 4 of the listed structures are forebrain parts.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-119","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":119,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Multipolar neurons possess one axon and two or more dendrites","Bipolar neurons possess one axon and one dendrite","Unmyelinated nerve fibres have no association with Schwann cells","Myelinated nerve fibres are present in spinal and cranial nerves"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: this is the NCERT definition of a multipolar neuron.\n(2) Correct: this is the definition of a bipolar neuron.\n(3) Incorrect: an unmyelinated fibre is still enclosed by a Schwann cell. That Schwann cell simply does not form a myelin sheath around the axon.\n(4) Correct: NCERT states that myelinated fibres are found in spinal and cranial nerves.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-120","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":120,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"An area in the brain which is associated with strong emotions is","image":"","options":["Cerebral cortex","Cerebellum","Limbic system","Medulla"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The limbic system includes the amygdala and hippocampus, deep inside the cerebral hemispheres.\nWorking with the hypothalamus, it regulates sexual behaviour and strong emotional reactions such as excitement, pleasure, rage and fear.\nThe cerebral cortex is tempting, but it handles sensory, motor and association functions such as memory and communication, not the basic emotional responses.\nThe cerebellum coordinates movement, and the medulla controls breathing and heart rate.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 21 (MCQ Q6)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-121","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":121,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Wax secreting gland present in the ear canal is called","image":"","options":["Sweat gland","Prostate gland","Cowper’s gland","Ceruminous gland"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The skin of the external auditory canal (meatus) has fine hairs and ceruminous glands.\nCeruminous glands are modified sweat glands that secrete cerumen (earwax). The wax traps dust and protects the canal.\nOrdinary sweat glands secrete sweat, not wax.\nThe prostate and Cowper's glands are male reproductive accessory glands.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 21 (MCQ Q9)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-122","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":122,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"A stimulus is applied at site A on the polarised membrane of a resting axon. To which ion does the membrane at site A first become freely permeable?","image":"","options":["Potassium ions ($K^+$)","Sodium ions ($Na^+$)","Chloride ions ($Cl^-$)","Negatively charged proteins of the axoplasm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT: at the stimulated site the membrane becomes freely permeable to $Na^+$, which then rushes into the axon.\nThis rapid $Na^+$ influx is what starts depolarisation.\nThe rise in $K^+$ permeability comes later and causes repolarisation, not the first response.\nThe axonal membrane stays impermeable to the negatively charged proteins of the axoplasm.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-123","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":123,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"An impulse is traced through a multipolar neuron, starting from where it is received and ending where a chemical signal is released for the next cell. Which sequence is correct?","image":"","options":["Axon → cell body → dendrite → synaptic knob → synaptic vesicle","Dendrite → axon → cell body → synaptic vesicle → synaptic knob","Dendrite → cell body → axon → branched axon terminal → synaptic knob → release from synaptic vesicles","Cell body → dendrite → axon → synaptic knob → release from Nissl's granules"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Dendrites carry impulses towards the cell body.\nThe axon then carries the impulse away from the cell body to its branched distal end.\nEach branch ends in a synaptic knob, where synaptic vesicles release neurotransmitters.\nOption (1) reverses the direction of flow.\nOption (2) places the axon before the cell body.\nOption (4) wrongly makes Nissl's granules the source of the released chemical.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-124","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":124,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Myelinated nerve fibres are found in spinal and cranial nerves.\nStatement II: An unmyelinated nerve fibre is not associated with any Schwann cell.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT states that myelinated fibres occur in spinal and cranial nerves.\nStatement II is incorrect. An unmyelinated fibre is still enclosed by a Schwann cell, but that Schwann cell does not form a myelin sheath around the axon.\nUnmyelinated fibres are common in the autonomic and somatic neural systems.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-125","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":125,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The neural system and the endocrine system jointly coordinate and integrate organ activities so that the organs function in a synchronised fashion.\n(ii) The endocrine system integrates functions through point-to-point nerve connections.\n(iii) Hydra possesses a brain along with a number of ganglia.\n(iv) Neurons can detect, receive and transmit different kinds of stimuli.\n(v) During physical exercise, the functions of the kidney are also coordinated along with those of muscles, lungs and heart.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: both systems jointly coordinate organ activities.\n(ii) Incorrect: the endocrine system acts chemically through hormones; point-to-point connections belong to the neural system.\n(iii) Incorrect: Hydra has only a network of neurons; a brain with ganglia is the insect pattern.\n(iv) Correct: this is the NCERT description of neurons.\n(v) Correct: NCERT lists the kidney among the organs coordinated during exercise.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-126","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":126,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a myelinated nerve fibre, the myelin sheath covers the axon continuously, with no interruption along its entire length.\nStatement II: Myelinated nerve fibres are found in spinal and cranial nerves.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is false. The myelin sheath is interrupted at gaps between adjacent sheaths, called nodes of Ranvier.\nStatement II is true. NCERT states that myelinated fibres are found in spinal and cranial nerves.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-127","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":127,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Afferent nerve fibres of the peripheral neural system carry regulatory impulses from the CNS to the peripheral tissues and organs.\nStatement II: The somatic neural system relays impulses from the CNS to skeletal muscles.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Afferent fibres carry impulses from tissues and organs to the CNS. Efferent fibres carry regulatory impulses from the CNS to the periphery.\nStatement II is correct. The somatic neural system relays impulses from the CNS to skeletal muscles. The autonomic system serves involuntary organs and smooth muscles.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-128","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":128,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Resting membrane potential is maintained by","image":"","options":["Hormones","Neurotransmitters","Ion pump","None of the above"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The ionic gradients across the resting axon membrane are kept by the sodium-potassium pump, which uses ATP.\nIt moves 3 Na$^+$ out for every 2 K$^+$ in. This keeps the outside positive and the inside negative, so the resting potential is maintained.\nNeurotransmitters act only at synapses to start a new impulse. Hormones act through the blood. Neither maintains the resting potential.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 21 (MCQ Q3)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-129","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":129,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Hypothalamus\n(B) Medulla oblongata\n(C) Corpus callosum\n(D) Corpora quadrigemina\nList-II:\n(I) Four round swellings on the dorsal side of the midbrain\n(II) Tract of nerve fibres joining the two cerebral hemispheres\n(III) Centres controlling respiration and cardiovascular reflexes\n(IV) Centres controlling body temperature and the urge for eating and drinking","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-IV: The hypothalamus contains centres for body temperature, eating and drinking.\nB-III: The medulla has centres for respiration, cardiovascular reflexes and gastric secretion.\nC-II: The corpus callosum is the fibre tract that connects the left and right hemispheres.\nD-I: The corpora quadrigemina are four lobes on the dorsal part of the midbrain.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-130","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":130,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"In the human peripheral neural system, the afferent nerve fibres carry impulses:","image":"","options":["from the CNS to skeletal muscles only","from tissues and organs to the CNS","from the CNS to involuntary organs and smooth muscles","between the two cerebral hemispheres"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT divides PNS fibres into afferent and efferent fibres.\nAfferent fibres carry impulses from tissues and organs towards the CNS, so option (2) is correct.\nOptions (1) and (3) describe efferent pathways, which carry impulses away from the CNS. They correspond to the somatic and autonomic outputs.\nOption (4) describes the corpus callosum, which lies within the brain and is not a PNS fibre.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-131","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":131,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"The sodium-potassium pump keeps up the ionic gradients across the resting axonal membrane. In each cycle it moves:","image":"","options":["2 $Na^+$ outwards for 3 $K^+$ into the cell","3 $K^+$ outwards for 2 $Na^+$ into the cell","3 $Na^+$ outwards for 2 $K^+$ into the cell","1 $Na^+$ outwards for 1 $K^+$ into the cell"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The pump works by active transport.\nIn each cycle it pushes 3 $Na^+$ out of the axon and brings 2 $K^+$ in.\nOption (1) reverses the numbers, and option (2) reverses the direction of both ions.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-132","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":132,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"The part of internal ear responsible for hearing is","image":"","options":["Cochlea","Semicircular canal","Utriculus","Sacculus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key NCERT fact: the internal ear (labyrinth) has two parts. The cochlea handles hearing. The vestibular apparatus handles balance.\nThe coiled cochlea contains the organ of Corti. Its hair cells turn sound vibrations into nerve impulses, which travel along the auditory nerve to the brain. So the cochlea is the part responsible for hearing.\nThe semicircular canals detect rotational (dynamic) movement. The utricle and saccule (macula) detect static equilibrium. All three are concerned with balance, not hearing.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 21 (MCQ Q10)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-133","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":133,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Read the two statements below.\nStatement I: At a chemical synapse, the specific receptors for neurotransmitters are present on the post-synaptic membrane.\nStatement II: Electrical synapses are rare in the human neural system.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Neurotransmitters released into the cleft bind to their specific receptors on the post-synaptic membrane. \nStatement II is correct. NCERT states that electrical synapses are rare in our system. Most synapses in our body are chemical. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-134","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":134,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Human eyeball consists of three layers and it encloses","image":"","options":["Lens, iris, optic nerve","Lens, aqueous humor and vitreous humor","Cornea, lens, iris","Cornea, lens, optic nerve"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The eyeball wall has three layers: the outer sclera (with the cornea in front), the middle choroid (with the ciliary body and iris) and the inner retina.\nThese layers enclose the lens, the aqueous humor (the space between cornea and lens) and the vitreous humor (the space behind the lens).\nOptions with cornea or iris are wrong because these are parts of the layers themselves, not structures enclosed by them.\nThe optic nerve leaves the eye, so it is not enclosed either.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 21 (MCQ Q8)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-135","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":135,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) Corpora quadrigemina\n(b) Pons\n(c) Cerebellum\n(d) Medulla oblongata\nList-II\n(i) Fibre tracts interconnecting different brain regions\n(ii) Centres controlling gastric secretions\n(iii) Four round swellings on the dorsal midbrain\n(iv) Integrates information from semicircular canals and the auditory system","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Corpora quadrigemina are the four round lobes on the dorsal midbrain, so (a)-(iii).\n(b) The pons consists of fibre tracts that interconnect brain regions, so (b)-(i).\n(c) The cerebellum integrates information from the semicircular canals and the auditory system, so (c)-(iv).\n(d) The medulla has centres for gastric secretions, respiration and cardiovascular reflexes, so (d)-(ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-136","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":136,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Which combination correctly describes the axoplasm of a resting axon?","image":"","options":["High $K^+$, high negatively charged proteins, low $Na^+$","High $Na^+$, low $K^+$, high negatively charged proteins","Low $K^+$, low $Na^+$, low negatively charged proteins","High $Na^+$, high $K^+$, no proteins"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The membrane is impermeable to the negatively charged proteins of the axoplasm, so they stay inside.\nThe pump keeps $K^+$ high and $Na^+$ low inside the axon.\nTherefore the axoplasm has high $K^+$, high negatively charged proteins and low $Na^+$.\nHigh $Na^+$ is a feature of the fluid outside the axon, which rules out options (2) and (4).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-137","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":137,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Each branch at the distal end of an axon ends in a bulb-like structure that holds vesicles filled with neurotransmitters. This structure is called the:","image":"","options":["Node of Ranvier","Nissl's granule","Synaptic knob","Schwann cell"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The distal end of the axon is branched.\nEach branch ends as a bulb-like synaptic knob.\nThe synaptic knob contains synaptic vesicles filled with neurotransmitters.\nA node of Ranvier is only the gap between two adjacent myelin sheaths. A Schwann cell forms the myelin sheath. Nissl's granules lie in the cell body and dendrites.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-138","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":138,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"A toxin specifically blocks the fusion of synaptic vesicles with the membrane of synaptic knobs at a neuro-muscular junction. All other parts of the neuron work normally. Which outcome is most likely?","image":"","options":["Impulses can no longer travel along the axon towards the synaptic knob","Dendrites stop conducting impulses towards the cell body","Nissl's granules disappear from the cell body, so the neuron dies","The impulse reaches the synaptic knob, but neurotransmitter is not released, so the muscle is not stimulated"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The toxin acts only at the synaptic knob, so dendrites, the cell body and axonal conduction are unaffected.\nThe impulse therefore still travels along the axon and reaches the knob.\nNeurotransmitters are released only when synaptic vesicles fuse with the membrane.\nWith fusion blocked, no neurotransmitter reaches the muscle, so the signal is not passed on.\nOption (1) is wrong because axonal conduction is unaffected.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-139","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":139,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about a resting (non-conducting) axon is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The axonal membrane is comparatively more permeable to $K^+$ than to $Na^+$","The sodium-potassium pump moves 3 $Na^+$ outwards for every 2 $K^+$ moved into the cell","The outer surface of the axonal membrane carries a positive charge","The axoplasm has a high concentration of $Na^+$ and a low concentration of $K^+$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: at rest the membrane is more permeable to $K^+$ and nearly impermeable to $Na^+$.\n(2) Correct: the pump exports 3 $Na^+$ and imports 2 $K^+$.\n(3) Correct: the outer surface is positive and the inner surface negative, so the membrane is polarised.\n(4) Incorrect: the axoplasm has high $K^+$, negatively charged proteins and low $Na^+$. High $Na^+$ is found outside the axon.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-140","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":140,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The axon transmits nerve impulses away from the cell body to a synapse or to a neuro-muscular junction.\nStatement II: Neurotransmitters are stored in the Nissl's granules of the cell body and released from there into the synaptic cleft.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. This is how NCERT describes the role of the axon.\nStatement II is incorrect. Neurotransmitters are held in synaptic vesicles inside the synaptic knobs at the axon terminals.\nNissl's granules are granular bodies in the cell body and dendrites, and they do not store neurotransmitters.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-141","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":141,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the parts of the human brain is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Pons and medulla oblongata belong to both the hindbrain and the brain stem","The cerebellum is a part of the hindbrain but not of the brain stem","The midbrain is a part of the brain stem but not of the hindbrain","All three regions of the brain stem belong to the hindbrain"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the hindbrain includes the pons and medulla, and both also belong to the brain stem.\n(2) Correct: the cerebellum is in the hindbrain but is not a brain stem region.\n(3) Correct: the midbrain is a separate division from the hindbrain, yet it forms part of the brain stem.\n(4) Incorrect: the brain stem includes the midbrain, which is not part of the hindbrain.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-142","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":142,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Synaptic knobs are present at the branched distal end of the axon and not at the tips of dendrites.\nReason (R): Dendrites transmit nerve impulses away from the cell body.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT describes the distal end of the axon as branched, with each branch ending in a synaptic knob.\nR is false. Dendrites carry impulses towards the cell body.\nIt is the axon that carries impulses away from the cell body to a synapse or neuromuscular junction.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-143","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":143,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about a resting (non-conducting) axon is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The axonal membrane is comparatively more permeable to $K^+$ than to $Na^+$","The axoplasm has a high concentration of $K^+$ and negatively charged proteins","The sodium-potassium pump moves 2 $Na^+$ outwards for every 3 $K^+$ brought into the cell","The outer surface of the axonal membrane is positively charged and the inner surface is negatively charged"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: at rest the membrane is more permeable to $K^+$ and nearly impermeable to $Na^+$.\n(2) Correct: the axoplasm holds high $K^+$, negatively charged proteins and low $Na^+$.\n(3) Incorrect: the ratio is reversed. The pump expels 3 $Na^+$ for every 2 $K^+$ taken in.\n(4) Correct: this charge distribution is the polarised state that gives the resting potential.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-144","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":144,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nA. Afferent fibres\nB. Efferent fibres\nC. Somatic neural system\nD. Autonomic neural system\nList-II\n(i) Relays impulses from CNS to skeletal muscles\n(ii) Carry impulses from tissues/organs to CNS\n(iii) Transmits impulses from CNS to involuntary organs and smooth muscles\n(iv) Carry regulatory impulses from CNS to peripheral tissues/organs\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A: Afferent fibres carry impulses towards the CNS, from tissues and organs, so A-(ii).\nB: Efferent fibres carry regulatory impulses away from the CNS to the periphery, so B-(iv).\nC: The somatic neural system supplies skeletal muscles, so C-(i).\nD: The autonomic neural system supplies involuntary organs and smooth muscles, so D-(iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-145","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":145,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Each branch at the distal end of an axon ends in a bulb-like structure called the","image":"","options":["synaptic knob","node of Ranvier","dendrite","Nissl's body"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The axon is a long fibre whose distal end is branched.\nEach branch ends as a bulb-like synaptic knob that carries synaptic vesicles.\nA node of Ranvier is a gap between two adjacent myelin sheaths along the axon, not a terminal swelling.\nDendrites arise from the cell body, not from the end of the axon.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-146","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":146,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The cerebral cortex is referred to as grey matter.\nReason (R): Neuron cell bodies are concentrated in the cerebral cortex.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The cerebral cortex has a greyish appearance and is therefore called grey matter.\nR is true. NCERT states that neuron cell bodies are concentrated in the cortex, and this gives it its colour. So R directly explains A.\nFor contrast, the white matter is made of myelinated fibre tracts lying in the inner part of the hemisphere.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-147","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":147,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"In a diagram of an axon, site A has just been stimulated and is depolarised. Site B, immediately ahead of A, is still in the resting (polarised) state. Which option correctly describes the local current flow between A and B?","image":"","options":["Inner surface: B to A; outer surface: A to B","Both inner and outer surfaces: A to B","Both inner and outer surfaces: B to A","Inner surface: A to B; outer surface: B to A"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"At depolarised site A, the inside is positive and the outside is negative.\nAt resting site B, the inside is negative and the outside is positive.\nCurrent flows from positive to negative, so on the inner surface it flows from A to B.\nOn the outer surface it flows from B to A, which completes the circuit.\nThis current reverses the polarity at B and generates an action potential there.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-148","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":148,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The brain stem forms the connection between the brain and the spinal cord.\nReason (R): The brain stem is made up of the midbrain, pons and cerebellum.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that the brain stem connects the brain with the spinal cord.\nR is false. The three regions of the brain stem are the midbrain, pons and medulla oblongata. The cerebellum is part of the hindbrain but is not included in the brain stem.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-149","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":149,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"When a neuron is resting, its axonal membrane is comparatively more permeable to which ion?","image":"","options":["Sodium ions ($Na^+$)","Potassium ions ($K^+$)","Chloride ions ($Cl^-$)","Calcium ions ($Ca^{2+}$)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT: at rest, the axonal membrane is comparatively more permeable to $K^+$.\nIt is nearly impermeable to $Na^+$, so $Na^+$ is the most tempting wrong choice.\nThe chapter does not discuss resting permeability to $Cl^-$ or $Ca^{2+}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-150","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":150,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the human brain is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The hypothalamus, a part of the forebrain, contains centres controlling body temperature and the urge for eating and drinking.","The brain controls the functioning of vital involuntary organs such as the lungs, heart and kidneys.","The midbrain lies between the pons and the medulla of the hindbrain.","The pia mater is the innermost cranial meninx and lies in contact with brain tissue."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the hypothalamus is in the forebrain and controls body temperature, hunger and thirst.\n(2) Correct: control of vital involuntary organs (lungs, heart, kidneys) is listed among the brain's functions.\n(3) Incorrect: the midbrain lies between the thalamus/hypothalamus (forebrain) and the pons. The pons and medulla are both hindbrain parts, and the midbrain does not sit between them.\n(4) Correct: the pia mater is the inner layer and contacts the brain tissue.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-151","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":151,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"The cerebrum wraps around a structure that acts as a major coordinating centre for sensory and motor signalling. This structure is the:","image":"","options":["Medulla oblongata","Hippocampus","Amygdala","Thalamus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"According to NCERT, the cerebrum wraps around the thalamus.\nThe thalamus is a major coordinating centre for sensory and motor signalling.\nThe hippocampus and amygdala are deep structures of the limbic system, which is concerned with emotions and motivation.\nThe medulla oblongata is in the hindbrain and controls respiration and cardiovascular reflexes.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-152","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":152,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about a resting neuron is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Ion channels on the neural membrane are selectively permeable to different ions.","The fluid outside the axon contains a high concentration of $K^+$ and a low concentration of $Na^+$.","The resting axonal membrane is impermeable to the negatively charged proteins of the axoplasm.","The electrical potential difference across the resting plasma membrane is called the resting potential."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the neural membrane has different ion channels, each selectively permeable to particular ions.\n(2) Incorrect: the outside fluid has LOW $K^+$ and HIGH $Na^+$. The statement reverses this.\n(3) Correct: the proteins cannot cross the membrane, so they stay in the axoplasm.\n(4) Correct: this is the NCERT definition of resting potential.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-153","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":153,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"In a sagittal section of the human brain, which cranial meningeal layer is in direct contact with the brain tissue?","image":"","options":["Dura mater","Arachnoid","Pia mater","Corpus callosum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The brain inside the skull is covered by three cranial meninges.\nFrom outside to inside they are the dura mater (outer), the arachnoid (very thin, middle) and the pia mater (inner).\nThe pia mater is the innermost layer, so it touches the brain tissue.\nDura mater is tempting because it is the best-known layer, but it is the outermost one.\nThe corpus callosum is a nerve tract joining the cerebral hemispheres, not a meninx.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-154","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":154,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"In a myelinated nerve fibre, the myelin sheath that envelops the axon is formed by:","image":"","options":["Nissl's granules","Schwann cells","Synaptic knobs","Dendrites"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that myelinated nerve fibres are enveloped by Schwann cells, which form a myelin sheath around the axon.\nNissl's granules are granular bodies in the cell body and dendrites.\nSynaptic knobs are the bulb-like ends of axon branches that hold neurotransmitter vesicles.\nDendrites are short branched fibres that carry impulses towards the cell body.\nNone of these three forms the myelin sheath.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-155","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":155,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"A chemical blocks the specific neurotransmitter receptors on the post-synaptic membrane of a chemical synapse. It does not affect the pre-synaptic neuron in any way. When an action potential reaches the axon terminal, what is the most likely result?","image":"","options":["The synaptic vesicles fail to fuse with the pre-synaptic membrane, so no neurotransmitter is released","Neurotransmitter is released into the cleft, but the post-synaptic ion channels do not open, so no new potential develops","Neurotransmitter binds normally, but the post-synaptic neuron develops only an inhibitory potential","Current flows directly between the two neurons, as it does at an electrical synapse"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The pre-synaptic neuron is unaffected. So the arriving action potential still moves the vesicles to the membrane, they fuse, and the neurotransmitter is released. This rules out option (1). \nWith its receptors blocked, the neurotransmitter cannot bind. Binding is what opens the ion channels on the post-synaptic membrane. \nNo channel opening means no ion entry, so no new potential is generated, whether excitatory or inhibitory. This rules out option (3). \nThe membranes are still separated by the cleft, so current cannot pass directly between them. This rules out option (4). \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-156","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":156,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Negatively charged proteins accumulate in the axoplasm of a resting neuron.\nReason (R): The resting axonal membrane is impermeable to these negatively charged proteins.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: the axoplasm contains a high concentration of negatively charged proteins.\nR is true: NCERT states that the membrane is impermeable to these proteins.\nBecause the proteins cannot leave, they remain trapped inside. This adds to the negative charge of the inner surface, so R directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-157","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":157,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"A sagittal section of the human brain shows a region with these features:\nAbove it lie the thalamus and hypothalamus, and below it lies the pons.\nA narrow canal runs through it.\nIts dorsal side bears four round swellings.\nIdentify this region.","image":"","options":["Medulla oblongata","Cerebellum","Midbrain","Hypothalamus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The midbrain lies between the thalamus/hypothalamus of the forebrain and the pons of the hindbrain.\nThe cerebral aqueduct passes through it.\nIts dorsal portion carries four lobes, the corpora quadrigemina.\nThe hypothalamus lies above this region, and the medulla lies below the pons.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-158","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":158,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following events of impulse transmission at a chemical synapse in the correct sequence:\n(a) Neurotransmitters bind to specific receptors\n(b) Action potential arrives at the axon terminal\n(c) Synaptic vesicles move towards and fuse with the plasma membrane\n(d) Neurotransmitters are released into the synaptic cleft\n(e) Ion channels open and a new potential develops in the post-synaptic neuron","image":"","options":["(b), (d), (c), (a), (e)","(c), (b), (d), (a), (e)","(b), (c), (d), (a), (e)","(b), (c), (a), (d), (e)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Step 1 (b): The action potential reaches the axon terminal.\nStep 2 (c): This stimulates the vesicles to move towards the membrane and fuse with it.\nStep 3 (d): Fusion releases the neurotransmitters into the cleft.\nStep 4 (a): The transmitters bind to receptors on the post-synaptic membrane.\nStep 5 (e): Binding opens ion channels, and ion entry generates a new potential.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-159","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":159,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Dendrites transmit nerve impulses towards the cell body of a neuron.\nReason (R): Dendrites contain Nissl's granules.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that dendrites transmit impulses towards the cell body.\nR is true: dendrites are short, branched fibres that contain Nissl's granules.\nR does not explain A, because Nissl's granules are not what sets the direction of impulse conduction.\nSo both statements are correct, but they are independent facts.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-160","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":160,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"The somatic neural system relays impulses from the CNS to","image":"","options":["smooth muscles of the gut","involuntary organs such as the heart","skeletal muscles","the viscera through plexuses"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The PNS is divided into the somatic and the autonomic neural systems.\nThe somatic neural system relays impulses from the CNS to skeletal muscles.\nImpulses to involuntary organs and smooth muscles are carried by the autonomic neural system. This rules out options (1) and (2).\nPathways to the viscera through plexuses belong to the visceral nervous system, which rules out option (4).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-161","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":161,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"In a myelinated nerve fibre, the gaps between two adjacent myelin sheaths are called:","image":"","options":["Synaptic clefts","Nissl's granules","Nodes of Ranvier","Synaptic knobs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Myelinated fibres are wrapped by Schwann cells, which form a myelin sheath around the axon.\nThe sheath is not continuous. The gaps between adjacent sheaths are the nodes of Ranvier.\nA synaptic cleft is the gap between two neurons, not a gap along one axon.\nSynaptic knobs are the bulb-like endings of axon branches.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-162","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":162,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The midbrain is located between the thalamus/hypothalamus of the forebrain and the pons of the hindbrain.\nReason (R): A canal called the cerebral aqueduct passes through the midbrain.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true: the midbrain lies between the thalamus/hypothalamus (forebrain) and the pons (hindbrain).\nR is true: the cerebral aqueduct passes through the midbrain.\nHowever, a canal running through the midbrain does not explain where the midbrain is located. The two facts are independent.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-163","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":163,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"The sodium-potassium pump maintains the ionic gradients across a resting axonal membrane. In one cycle, the pump moves:","image":"","options":["3 $Na^+$ out of the cell and 2 $K^+$ into the cell","2 $Na^+$ out of the cell and 3 $K^+$ into the cell","3 $Na^+$ into the cell and 2 $K^+$ out of the cell","2 $Na^+$ into the cell and 3 $K^+$ out of the cell"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The pump works by active transport against the concentration gradients.\nIt sends $Na^+$ outward, where $Na^+$ is already high, and brings $K^+$ inward, where $K^+$ is already high.\nThe ratio is 3 $Na^+$ out for every 2 $K^+$ in.\nOption (2) reverses the numbers. Options (3) and (4) reverse the directions.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-164","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":164,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Which sequence correctly arranges the cranial meninges from the skull inwards towards the brain?","image":"","options":["Pia mater → Arachnoid → Dura mater","Dura mater → Arachnoid → Pia mater","Arachnoid → Dura mater → Pia mater","Dura mater → Pia mater → Arachnoid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The dura mater is the outer layer and lies just beneath the skull.\nThe arachnoid is the very thin middle layer.\nThe pia mater is the inner layer and lies on the brain tissue.\nSo, going from outside to inside, the order is dura mater, arachnoid, pia mater.\nOption (1) gives the same layers in reverse, from inside to outside.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-165","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":165,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the human neural system are correct?\n(i) The PNS comprises all the nerves of the body associated with the brain and spinal cord.\n(ii) The spinal cord is a part of the peripheral neural system.\n(iii) The autonomic neural system transmits impulses from the CNS to involuntary organs and smooth muscles.\n(iv) In the visceral nervous system, impulses travel only from the CNS to the viscera, never in the reverse direction.\n(v) The somatic neural system relays impulses from the CNS to the smooth muscles of the gut.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NCERT defines the PNS as all the nerves associated with the brain and spinal cord.\n(ii) Incorrect: The spinal cord is part of the CNS.\n(iii) Correct: This is the NCERT role of the autonomic neural system.\n(iv) Incorrect: Visceral pathways carry impulses from the CNS to the viscera and also from the viscera to the CNS.\n(v) Incorrect: Smooth muscles are supplied by the autonomic system; the somatic system supplies skeletal muscles.\nSo two statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-166","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":166,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"A drug completely blocks the sodium-potassium pump of an axon, but its ion channels keep working normally. If the axon is observed over a long time, which outcome is expected?","image":"","options":["The $Na^+$ and $K^+$ concentration gradients gradually run down, so the resting potential can no longer be maintained","The axoplasm gains more $K^+$ and loses $Na^+$, so the membrane becomes more strongly polarised","The membrane immediately becomes impermeable to $K^+$ and remains permanently depolarised","The negatively charged proteins move out of the axoplasm, making the inner surface positive"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the ionic gradients across the resting membrane are maintained by the pump's active transport.\nWithout the pump, ions keep moving down their gradients through the channels. Nothing restores them, so the gradients slowly dissipate. The resting potential that depends on them then fails.\nOption (2) wrongly assumes ions keep moving against their gradients, which requires the pump.\nOption (4) fails because the membrane is impermeable to these proteins.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-167","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":167,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the resting and action potentials of a neuron is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["In the resting state, the axoplasm has a high concentration of $K^+$ and negatively charged proteins","In the resting state, the axonal membrane is nearly impermeable to $Na^+$","After depolarisation, the resting potential at the excited site is restored mainly by a further rapid influx of $Na^+$","During an action potential, the inner surface of the membrane at the stimulated site becomes positively charged"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the resting axoplasm has a high concentration of $K^+$ and of negatively charged proteins.\n(2) Correct: at rest, the membrane is more permeable to $K^+$ and nearly impermeable to $Na^+$.\n(3) Incorrect: the rise in $Na^+$ permeability is short-lived. Repolarisation follows a rise in $K^+$ permeability, with $K^+$ diffusing outward.\n(4) Correct: $Na^+$ influx reverses the polarity, so the inside of the membrane becomes positive.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-168","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":168,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Immediately after the rapid influx of $Na^+$ at a stimulated site of an axon, the charges on the two surfaces of the membrane at that site are:","image":"","options":["Outer surface positive, inner surface negative","Both surfaces positive","Outer surface negative, inner surface positive","Both surfaces electrically neutral"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"At rest, the outer surface of the axonal membrane is positive and the inner surface is negative.\nThe $Na^+$ influx reverses this polarity, so the outer surface becomes negative and the inner surface becomes positive.\nThis reversed state is called depolarisation.\nOption (1) describes the resting (polarised) state, not the stimulated site.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-169","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":169,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Together with the hypothalamus, the limbic system is involved in regulating sexual behaviour and in expressing emotions such as excitement, pleasure, rage and fear.\nStatement II: The limbic system is formed by the outer folded layer of the cerebral cortex, with no contribution from deep structures.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. NCERT says the limbic system, along with the hypothalamus, regulates sexual behaviour, emotional reactions (excitement, pleasure, rage, fear) and motivation.\nStatement II is false. The limbic system (limbic lobe) is formed by the inner parts of the cerebral hemispheres together with deep structures such as the amygdala and hippocampus. It is not formed by the outer cortex.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-170","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":170,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Centres that control body temperature and the urge for eating and drinking are located in the:","image":"","options":["Thalamus","Cerebellum","Corpus callosum","Hypothalamus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The hypothalamus lies at the base of the thalamus. It contains centres that control body temperature and the urge to eat and drink.\nThe thalamus is a tempting choice because it is nearby. However, it is a coordinating centre for sensory and motor signalling, not a centre for hunger or temperature.\nThe cerebellum belongs to the hindbrain, and the corpus callosum only connects the two hemispheres.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-171","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":171,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"The ionic gradients across a resting axonal membrane are maintained by the sodium-potassium pump. In one cycle, this pump moves:","image":"","options":["3 $Na^+$ out of the cell and 2 $K^+$ into the cell","2 $Na^+$ out of the cell and 3 $K^+$ into the cell","3 $Na^+$ into the cell and 2 $K^+$ out of the cell","2 $Na^+$ into the cell and 2 $K^+$ out of the cell"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the sodium-potassium pump actively moves 3 $Na^+$ outwards for every 2 $K^+$ brought into the cell.\nThis unequal exchange helps keep the outer surface positive and the inner surface negative.\nOption (2) reverses the numbers.\nOption (3) reverses the directions of movement.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-172","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":172,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about nerve impulse conduction along an axon are correct?\n(i) A stimulus makes the membrane at the stimulated site freely permeable to $Na^+$.\n(ii) The stimulus-induced rise in $Na^+$ permeability persists until the impulse reaches the axon terminal.\n(iii) While site A is depolarised, the outer surface of the membrane at site B just ahead is still positively charged.\n(iv) On the outer surface of the membrane, current flows from the depolarised site A to site B.\n(v) Repolarisation at a site involves outward diffusion of $K^+$.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the stimulated site becomes freely permeable to $Na^+$.\n(ii) Incorrect: the rise in $Na^+$ permeability is extremely short-lived.\n(iii) Correct: site B is still polarised, with its outer surface positive.\n(iv) Incorrect: on the outer surface, current flows from B to A.\n(v) Correct: $K^+$ diffuses out and restores the resting potential.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-173","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":173,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The neural system brings about quick coordination of body activities.\nReason (R): The neural system integrates organs by releasing hormones into the blood.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The neural system provides quick coordination through an organised network of point-to-point connections.\nR is false. Releasing hormones for chemical integration is the role of the endocrine system, not the neural system.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-174","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":174,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The dura mater is the outermost layer of the cranial meninges.\nStatement II: The arachnoid is the meningeal layer that is in direct contact with the brain tissue.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The cranial meninges are arranged from outside to inside as dura mater, then arachnoid, then pia mater.\nStatement I is correct, because the dura mater is the outer layer.\nStatement II is incorrect. The arachnoid is the very thin middle layer. The pia mater is the inner layer in contact with the brain tissue.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-175","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":175,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"A neuron whose cell body bears only one axon and no dendrites is termed ______ and, according to NCERT, is found usually in the ______.","image":"","options":["bipolar; retina","multipolar; cerebral cortex","unipolar; adult spinal cord","unipolar; embryonic stage"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A cell body with one axon only and no dendrites defines a unipolar neuron.\nNCERT states that such neurons are found usually in the embryonic stage.\nOption (3) has the correct name but the wrong location.\nBipolar neurons (retina) and multipolar neurons (cerebral cortex) both have dendrites.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-176","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":176,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Inside the skull, the brain is covered by three cranial meninges. Which layer is in direct contact with the brain tissue?","image":"","options":["Dura mater","Arachnoid","Periosteum","Pia mater"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The cranial meninges, from outside to inside, are dura mater, arachnoid and pia mater.\nThe inner layer, pia mater, is in contact with the brain tissue.\nDura mater is the outermost layer, so it is not touching the brain.\nArachnoid is the very thin middle layer.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-177","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":177,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The inner part of each cerebral hemisphere has an opaque white appearance.\nReason (R): The fibres of the tracts in this region are covered with myelin sheath.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: the inner part of the cerebral hemisphere forms the white matter.\nR is true: the fibre tracts in this region are covered with myelin sheath.\nThe myelin is what gives the layer its opaque white look. So R directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-178","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":178,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about synapses are correct?\n(a) Electrical synapses are the most common type of synapse in the human body.\n(b) Transmission across an electrical synapse is very similar to impulse conduction along a single axon.\n(c) Vesicles containing neurotransmitters are present in the axon terminals.\n(d) The new potential developed in a post-synaptic neuron is always excitatory.\n(e) At a chemical synapse, the pre- and post-synaptic membranes are separated by a fluid-filled space.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Incorrect. Electrical synapses are rare in our system. \n(b) Correct. NCERT compares transmission at an electrical synapse to conduction along a single axon. \n(c) Correct. Axon terminals contain vesicles filled with neurotransmitters. \n(d) Incorrect. The new potential may be either excitatory or inhibitory. \n(e) Correct. This fluid-filled space is the synaptic cleft. \nSo three statements, (b), (c) and (e), are correct. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-179","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":179,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The midbrain lies between the forebrain and the hindbrain.\nReason (R): The dorsal portion of the midbrain consists mainly of four round swellings called corpora quadrigemina.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. The midbrain is located between the thalamus/hypothalamus (forebrain) and the pons (hindbrain).\nR is true. The dorsal midbrain bears four lobes, the corpora quadrigemina.\nHowever, R only describes the structure of the midbrain. It does not explain the midbrain's position between the forebrain and hindbrain.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-180","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":180,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Mark the vitamin present in Rhodopsin","image":"","options":["Vit A","Vit B","Vit C","Vit D"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The photopigment rhodopsin (visual purple) in rods is made of the protein opsin and retinal.\nRetinal is an aldehyde of vitamin A.\nLight changes the structure of retinal, which changes opsin and starts a nerve impulse.\nThis is why vitamin A deficiency causes night blindness.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 21 (MCQ Q7)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-181","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":181,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"In a chemical synapse, what is the fluid-filled space that separates the membrane of the pre-synaptic neuron from that of the post-synaptic neuron called?","image":"","options":["Node of Ranvier","Synaptic cleft","Synaptic knob","Neuro-muscular junction"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"At a chemical synapse, the pre- and post-synaptic membranes are separated by a fluid-filled space called the synaptic cleft. \nThe synaptic knob is the bulb-like end of an axon branch, so it lies on the pre-synaptic side. It is not the gap itself. \nA node of Ranvier is a gap between adjacent myelin sheaths on an axon. \nA neuro-muscular junction is a junction between a neuron and a muscle, not the space inside a neuron-to-neuron synapse. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-182","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":182,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"The sympathetic and parasympathetic neural systems are subdivisions of the:","image":"","options":["somatic neural system","central neural system","autonomic neural system","brain stem"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT divides the PNS into the somatic and autonomic neural systems.\nThe autonomic neural system is further classified into the sympathetic and parasympathetic neural systems, so option (3) is correct.\nThe somatic system is the most tempting wrong choice. It only relays impulses from the CNS to skeletal muscles and has no such subdivisions.\nThe CNS and the brain stem are parts of the central system, not divisions of the PNS.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-NEURAL-183","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Neural Control and Coordination","qno":183,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Neural Control and Coordination","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) Nissl's granules\n(b) Dendrites\n(c) Axon\n(d) Synaptic vesicles\nList-II:\n(i) Carries impulses away from the cell body to a synapse or neuromuscular junction\n(ii) Contain neurotransmitters\n(iii) Granular bodies in the cytoplasm of the cell body\n(iv) Short, repeatedly branched fibres conducting impulses towards the cell body","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii","a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i","a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii","a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Nissl's granules are granular bodies in the cell body cytoplasm, so a matches (iii).\n(b) Dendrites are short, branched fibres carrying impulses towards the cell body, so b matches (iv).\n(c) The axon carries impulses away from the cell body to a synapse or neuromuscular junction, so c matches (i).\n(d) Synaptic vesicles in the synaptic knobs contain neurotransmitters, so d matches (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 18 · Neural Control and Coordination","difficulty":"medium"}]});
